## Usage Examples & Tests

```typescript
import { groupBy, groupByToMap } from "./groupBy";

// Basic grouping
console.log(groupBy([1.1, 2.4, 1.9, 3.0], Math.floor));
// { '1': [1.1, 1.9], '2': [2.4], '3': [3.0] }

// String length
console.log(groupBy(["one", "two", "three"], s => s.length));
// { '3': ['one', 'two'], '5': ['three'] }

// Empty array returns an empty object
console.log(groupBy([], x => x));
// {}

// Dangerous keys are handled safely thanks to the null prototype
console.log(groupBy(["a", "b"], () => "__proto__"));
// { __proto__: ['a', 'b'] }  (stored as a real own property)

// Map version keeps 1 and "1" separate
const mixed = groupByToMap([1, "1", 1], x => x);
console.log(mixed.get(1));   // [1, 1]
console.log(mixed.get("1")); // ["1"]

// Quick assertions
import assert from "node:assert/strict";

assert.deepEqual(
  { ...groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4], n => (n % 2 ? "odd" : "even")) },
  { odd: [1, 3], even: [2, 4] }
);
assert.deepEqual({ ...groupBy([], x => x) }, {});
assert.throws(() => groupBy(null as any, x => x), TypeError);
assert.throws(() => groupBy([1], "nope" as any), TypeError);
console.log("All tests passed ✔");
```

## Design Notes

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **`Object.create(null)`** | Keys like `"toString"` or `"__proto__"` can't collide with inherited properties or cause prototype pollution. |
| **Single pass, `for` loop** | O(n), with no intermediate arrays as you'd get from `reduce` with spreading. |
| **Order preserved** | Items keep their original order within each group, and groups appear in first-seen order. |
| **`keyFn` receives `(item, index, array)`** | Matches the native array callback convention, which enables tricks like chunking by index. |
| **Input validation** | Fails fast with a clear message instead of a cryptic error deep inside the loop. |
| **Separate `groupByToMap`** | Plain objects coerce keys to strings. `Map` handles object keys and type-distinct keys correctly. |
| **Native fallback** | On ES2024+ runtimes, `Object.groupBy(arr, fn)` and `Map.groupBy(arr, fn)` are built-in equivalents. This utility serves older environments or when you want validation and index access. |

