## Design Notes

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| `Object.create(null)` | Prevents prototype pollution and odd behavior with keys like `"constructor"`. |
| Single `for` loop | One pass with O(n) time and no intermediate arrays, unlike `reduce` with spreading. |
| Passes `index` and `array` to `keyFn` | Matches the native array callback convention (`map`, `filter`, and so on). |
| Separate `Map` variant | Plain objects coerce keys to strings. `Map` supports any key type. |
| Input validation | Fails fast with clear messages instead of a cryptic error later. |

> **Native alternative:** Modern runtimes (ES2024, Node 21+, current browsers) provide `Object.groupBy(arr, keyFn)` and `Map.groupBy(arr, keyFn)` with the same semantics. Prefer those when your target environment supports them. This utility serves as a polyfill-style fallback.

Here's a well-documented `groupBy` implementation in JavaScript, with a TypeScript version further down.

## JavaScript Implementation

```javascript
/**
 * Groups the elements of an array into buckets keyed by the result of a
 * key-derivation function.
 *
 * Each element is passed to `keyFn`, and the returned value is used as the
 * key under which the element is stored. Elements that produce the same key
 * are collected into the same array, preserving their original relative order.
 *
 * The result is a plain object with a `null` prototype, so keys such as
 * "constructor", "toString", or "__proto__" are safe and won't collide with
 * inherited Object properties.
 *
 * @template T
 * @param {T[]} arr - The array of items to group. Must be an array (or array-like iterable).
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, array: T[]) => PropertyKey} keyFn
 *   A function that derives a grouping key for each item. It receives:
 *     - `item`  – the current element
 *     - `index` – the element's index in the array
 *     - `array` – the original array
 *   The returned key is coerced to a property key (string or symbol).
 *   Note: because object keys are strings, `1` and `"1"` map to the same group.
 *
 * @returns {Record<PropertyKey, T[]>} An object whose keys are the derived
 *   keys and whose values are arrays of the items that produced each key.
 *   Returns an empty object if `arr` is empty.
 *
 * @throws {TypeError} If `arr` is not iterable or `keyFn` is not a function.
 *
 * @example
 * // Group numbers by parity
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => (n % 2 === 0 ? 'even' : 'odd'));
 * // => { odd: [1, 3, 5], even: [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Group objects by a property
 * const people = [
 *   { name: 'Alice', dept: 'Engineering' },
 *   { name: 'Bob',   dept: 'Sales' },
 *   { name: 'Cara',  dept: 'Engineering' },
 * ];
 * groupBy(people, p => p.dept);
 * // => {
 * //   Engineering: [{ name: 'Alice', ... }, { name: 'Cara', ... }],
 * //   Sales:       [{ name: 'Bob', ... }]
 * // }
 *
 * @example
 * // Group strings by length
 * groupBy(['one', 'two', 'three'], s => s.length);
 * // => { 3: ['one', 'two'], 5: ['three'] }
 *
 * @see groupByMap — use this variant if you need non-string keys
 *   (e.g. objects) or want to distinguish `1` from `"1"`.
 */
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  if (arr == null || typeof arr[Symbol.iterator] !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: first argument must be an iterable');
  }
  if (typeof keyFn !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: keyFn must be a function');
  }

  const groups = Object.create(null);
  let index = 0;

  for (const item of arr) {
    const key = keyFn(item, index++, arr);
    // Push into the existing bucket, or create one on first sight of the key.
    (groups[key] ??= []).push(item);
  }

  return groups;
}

/**
 * Like {@link groupBy}, but returns a `Map` instead of a plain object.
 *
 * Use this when keys are not strings, for example objects, or when
 * `1` and `"1"` must be treated as distinct keys. Map keys are compared
 * using SameValueZero semantics, so object keys group by reference.
 *
 * @template T, K
 * @param {Iterable<T>} arr - The items to group.
 * @param {(item: T, index: number) => K} keyFn - Derives the key for each item.
 * @returns {Map<K, T[]>} A Map from key to the array of matching items,
 *   with keys in first-seen order.
 *
 * @example
 * groupByMap([1, '1', 2], x => x);
 * // => Map { 1 => [1], '1' => ['1'], 2 => [2] }
 */
function groupByMap(arr, keyFn) {
  if (typeof keyFn !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupByMap: keyFn must be a function');
  }

  const groups = new Map();
  let index = 0;

  for (const item of arr) {
    const key = keyFn(item, index++);
    const bucket = groups.get(key);
    if (bucket) {
      bucket.push(item);
    } else {
      groups.set(key, [item]);
    }
  }

  return groups;
}

module.exports = { groupBy, groupByMap };
```

## TypeScript Version

```typescript
/**
 * Groups array items by a key derived from each item.
 * See the JavaScript version above for full documentation.
 */
export function groupBy<T, K extends PropertyKey>(
  arr: Iterable<T>,
  keyFn: (item: T, index: number) => K
): Partial<Record<K, T[]>> {
  const groups = Object.create(null) as Partial<Record<K, T[]>>;
  let index = 0;
  for (const item of arr) {
    const key = keyFn(item, index++);
    (groups[key] ??= []).push(item);
  }
  return groups;
}
```

The return type is `Partial<Record<K, T[]>>` because not every possible `K` is guaranteed to appear. For example, with a `'odd' | 'even'` key, an all-odd array has no `even` bucket, so TypeScript will correctly make you handle `undefined`.

## Design Notes

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Null-prototype object** | Avoids bugs when a key is `"constructor"` or `"__proto__"`. |
| **Order preserved** | Items appear in each group in their original order, and groups appear in first-seen key order (for non-integer keys). |
| **Index passed to `keyFn`** | Enables position-based grouping, e.g. chunking with `(_, i) => Math.floor(i / 3)`. |
| **Single pass, O(n)** | Each item is visited once. |
| **Input validation** | Fails fast with a clear message instead of an obscure runtime error. |
| **`Map` variant** | Plain objects stringify keys, so the Map version is offered for object keys or type-sensitive keys. |

