## Key design decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Doesn't throw on HTTP errors** | Matches native `fetch` semantics; callers check `response.ok`. |
| **Selective status retries** | 4xx errors like 400/404 won't succeed on retry, so only transient codes (429, 5xx, etc.) are retried. |
| **Full jitter by default** | Prevents the "thundering herd" problem where many clients retry at the same moment ([AWS recommends this](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/exponential-backoff-and-jitter/)). |
| **Honors `Retry-After`** | Servers that rate-limit you usually say how long to wait, so that value takes priority. |
| **Separate timeout vs. user abort** | A timeout is retryable; a user-triggered abort cancels everything immediately, including pending sleeps. |
| **`maxDelay` cap** | Stops exponential growth from producing absurd waits. |
| **Cancels discarded bodies** | Frees the underlying connection for reuse. |

