 *   rethrown immediately.
 * - HTTP responses with status 408, 429, 500, 502, 503 or 504 are retried.
 *   Other responses (including other 4xx errors) are returned as-is, so the
 *   caller can inspect `response.ok` as with plain `fetch`.
 * - The delay before retry attempt n (0-based) is
 *   `min(maxDelay, baseDelay * 2^n)`, randomized by "full jitter"
 *   (a random value between 0 and that cap) to avoid thundering herds.
 * - If the server sends a `Retry-After` header, it is used in place of the
 *   computed delay (still capped by `maxDelay`).
 * - If all retries are exhausted on a retryable HTTP status, the last
 *   response is returned. If the final failure was a network error, it is thrown.
 *
 * Note: request bodies that are streams cannot be re-sent, so only use
 * retries with string, Blob, FormData, URLSearchParams or buffer bodies.
 * Retrying non-idempotent requests (e.g. POST) may cause duplicate effects.
 *
 * @param {string|URL|Request} url - The resource to fetch.
 * @param {RequestInit & {
 *   baseDelay?: number,
 *   maxDelay?: number,
 *   retryOn?: number[],
