 *   treated as equal to each other.
 *
 * @returns {Map<*, T[]>} A Map of key → array of items, in first-seen
 *   key order. Each item appears exactly once, in its original order
 *   within its group.
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy([6.1, 4.2, 6.3], Math.floor);
 * // => Map { 6 => [6.1, 6.3], 4 => [4.2] }
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy(
 *   ["apple", "avocado", "banana"],
 *   (fruit) => fruit[0]
 * );
 * // => Map { "a" => ["apple", "avocado"], "b" => ["banana"] }
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy(
 *   [{ type: "a", v: 1 }, { type: "b", v: 2 }, { type: "a", v: 3 }],
 *   (o) => o.type
 * );
 * // => Map { "a" => [{v:1},{v:3}], "b" => [{v:2}] }
 */
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  const result = new Map();

  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) {
    return result; // Gracefully handle non-array input
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i, arr);

    if (result.has(key)) {
      result.get(key).push(item);
    } else {
      result.set(key, [item]);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

## Design notes

- **Returns a `Map`, not a plain object** — this allows *any* value as a key (numbers, objects, `NaN`, etc.) and preserves insertion order.
- **`keyFn` signature matches `map`/`forEach`** — `(item, index, array)` — so it feels familiar and works with existing callbacks.
- **Stable ordering** — items within each group keep their original relative order; groups appear in first-seen order.
- **O(n) time complexity** — single pass with constant-time Map operations.

