# Cache

A simple in-memory key/value cache.

## API

### `new Cache()`
Creates a new, empty cache instance.

### `cache.get(key)` → `any | undefined`
Returns the value stored under `key`, or `undefined` if the key doesn't exist.

### `cache.set(key, value)`
Stores `value` under `key`, overwriting any existing value. Returns the cache instance (for chaining).

### `cache.has(key)` → `boolean`
Returns `true` if `key` exists in the cache, otherwise `false`.

### `cache.delete(key)` → `boolean`
Removes `key` from the cache. Returns `true` if the key existed and was removed, `false` otherwise.

## Implementation

```js
class Cache {
  constructor() {
    this._store = new Map();
  }

  /** Get the value for `key`, or undefined if not present. */
  get(key) {
    return this._store.get(key);
  }

  /** Store `value` under `key`. Returns the cache for chaining. */
  set(key, value) {
    this._store.set(key, value);
    return this;
  }

  /** Check whether `key` exists. */
  has(key) {
    return this._store.has(key);
  }

  /** Remove `key`. Returns true if it existed, false otherwise. */
  delete(key) {
    return this._store.delete(key);
  }
}
```

## Example

```js
const cache = new Cache();

cache.set("user:1", { name: "Ada" });

cache.has("user:1");   // true
cache.get("user:1");   // { name: "Ada" }
cache.delete("user:1"); // true
cache.get("user:1");   // undefined
```

**Notes:**
- Keys can be any type (the underlying `Map` allows primitives, objects, etc.).
- For a browser equivalent without `Map`, swap in a plain object with string keys (at the cost of losing non-string key support).

# Cache Class

A simple in-memory cache with optional TTL (time-to-live) support.

## API Documentation

### `new Cache()`
Creates a new empty cache instance.

### `get(key)` → `* | undefined`
Returns the cached value for `key`, or `undefined` if the key is absent or expired. Expired entries are removed on access.

### `set(key, value, [ttl])`
Stores `value` under `key`. Optionally accepts a `ttl` in milliseconds; the entry expires after that duration.

### `has(key)` → `boolean`
Returns `true` if the key exists and has not expired.

### `delete(key)` → `boolean`
Removes the entry for `key`. Returns `true` if an entry was deleted, `false` otherwise.

## Implementation

```js
class Cache {
  #entries = new Map();

  /**
   * Get the value for a key.
   * @param {string} key
   * @returns {*} The cached value, or undefined if missing/expired.
   */
  get(key) {
    const entry = this.#entries.get(key);
    if (!entry) return undefined;

    if (entry.expires !== null && Date.now() > entry.expires) {
      this.#entries.delete(key); // lazy expiration
      return undefined;
    }
    return entry.value;
  }

  /**
   * Store a value under a key.
   * @param {string} key
   * @param {*} value
   * @param {number} [ttl] - Time-to-live in milliseconds.
   */
  set(key, value, ttl) {
    const expires = ttl != null ? Date.now() + ttl : null;
    this.#entries.set(key, { value, expires });
    return this; // chainable
  }

  /**
   * Check whether a live (non-expired) entry exists.
   * @param {string} key
   * @returns {boolean}
   */
  has(key) {
    return this.get(key) !== undefined;
  }

  /**
   * Delete an entry.
   * @param {string} key
   * @returns {boolean} True if an entry was removed.
   */
  delete(key) {
    return this.#entries.delete(key);
  }
}
```

## Usage Example

```js
const cache = new Cache();

cache.set("user:42", { name: "Ada" });
cache.set("token", "abc", 5000); // expires in 5 seconds

cache.has("user:42");   // true
cache.get("user:42");   // { name: "Ada" }
cache.delete("user:42"); // true
cache.has("user:42");   // false
```

**Notes:**
- Expiration is lazy: entries are purged when accessed, so expired-but-unaccessed entries linger until read. For active cleanup, you could add a periodic sweep with `setInterval`.
- `has()` relies on `get()`, so it correctly treats expired entries as absent.

