```

### Notes

- Keys are coerced to JavaScript property keys. For example, a numeric key `30` becomes the string `"30"`.
- If you need to preserve key identity exactly, such as distinguishing `1` from `"1"` or using object keys, use a `Map` instead:

```js
function groupByMap(arr, keyFn) {
  const groups = new Map();

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i, arr);

    if (!groups.has(key)) {
      groups.set(key, []);
    }

    groups.get(key).push(item);
  }

  return groups;
}
```

- Because the returned object has a null prototype, it does not have methods like `hasOwnProperty`, `toString`, etc. Use `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(groups, key)` or `Object.hasOwn(groups, key)` when checking keys.

