A few notes on the design choices:

- **String coercion.** Object keys are always strings, so `keyFn` returning `1` and `"1"` will merge into the same group. If you need to preserve key identity (e.g. object keys, or distinguishing `1` from `"1"`), swap the return type to a `Map` and drop the `String(...)` call.
- **Null prototype.** Using `Object.create(null)` means a key of `"__proto__"` won't silently corrupt the result. If you need `hasOwnProperty` etc. on the result, use `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(groups, key)` instead of `groups.hasOwnProperty(key)`.
- **Single pass.** O(n) time, O(n) space. No intermediate arrays are created beyond the group buckets themselves.
- **No mutation.** The input array and its elements are never modified.

```ts
/**
 * Groups the elements of an array by a key derived from each element.
 *
 * The function makes a single pass over `arr`, so it runs in O(n) time
 * (plus the cost of `keyFn`). Elements that produce the same key are
 * collected into the same bucket, and their relative order inside that
 * bucket matches their order in the input.
 *
 * @template T  Element type of the input array.
 * @template K  Type of the derived key (string, number, or symbol).
 *
 * @param arr    The array to group. Not mutated.
 * @param keyFn  Called once per element with `(element, index)`; must
 *               return the bucket key for that element.
 *
 * @returns A null-prototype object whose own properties are the derived
 *          keys, each mapped to an array of the elements that produced it.
 *          The null prototype is deliberate: it means a key of `"__proto__"`
 *          (or any other `Object.prototype` member) becomes a normal
 *          property instead of corrupting the result object.
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => (n % 2 === 0 ? 'even' : 'odd'));
 * // => { odd: [1, 3, 5], even: [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy(['apple', 'avocado', 'banana'], s => s[0]);
 * // => { a: ['apple', 'avocado'], b: ['banana'] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Numeric keys are coerced to strings by the plain-object return type.
 * groupBy([1.5, 2.5, 1.25], n => Math.floor(n));
 * // => { '1': [1.5, 1.25], '2': [2.5] }
 *
 * @example
 * // The index is available as the second argument to `keyFn`.
 * groupBy(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], (_s, i) => (i < 2 ? 'first half' : 'second half'));
 * // => { 'first half': ['a', 'b'], 'second half': ['c', 'd'] }
 */
function groupBy<T, K extends PropertyKey>(
  arr: readonly T[],
  keyFn: (item: T, index: number) => K,
): Record<K, T[]> {
  const groups = Object.create(null) as Record<K, T[]>;

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i);

    if (!Object.hasOwn(groups, key)) {
      groups[key] = [];
    }
    groups[key].push(item);
  }

  return groups;
}
```

Notes:

- **Empty input** returns an empty object, never `null`/`undefined`.
- **Single pass**, no intermediate arrays, so it works on large inputs without extra allocation beyond the buckets themselves.
- **Null prototype** avoids prototype-pollution surprises. If you need a "normal" object, wrap the result with `{ ...groupBy(...) }`.
- **Symbol keys** work, since the result is an object and `Object.hasOwn` accepts `PropertyKey`.
- If you'd rather keep keys uncoerced (e.g. objects as keys), swap the internals for a `Map<K, T[]>` and return that instead — the API and semantics stay identical.

```js
/**
 * Groups the elements of an array into buckets keyed by a value derived
 * from each element.
 *
 * The returned value is a `Map` rather than a plain object so that:
 *   - keys keep their original type (numbers stay numbers, objects stay
 *     objects, `NaN` works, etc.) instead of being coerced to strings;
 *   - keys that collide with `Object.prototype` members (`"constructor"`,
 *     `"__proto__"`, …) are handled safely;
 *   - insertion order of keys is preserved.
 *
 * @template T
 * @template K
 *
 * @param {Iterable<T>} arr
 *   The items to group. Any iterable works (arrays, sets, generators, …).
 *
 * @param {(item: T, index: number) => K} keyFn
 *   Called once per item with the item and its 0-based position. The value
 *   it returns is used as the group key. Returning `undefined` or `null`
 *   is allowed — those items are simply grouped under that key.
 *
 * @returns {Map<K, T[]>}
 *   A map from each distinct key to the array of items that produced it,
 *   in the order they were encountered. Keys with no items never appear.
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => (n % 2 === 0 ? 'even' : 'odd'));
 * // Map { 'odd' => [1, 3, 5], 'even' => [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 * const people = [
 *   { name: 'Ada',   dept: 'eng' },
 *   { name: 'Grace', dept: 'eng' },
 *   { name: 'Linus', dept: 'ops' },
 * ];
 * groupBy(people, p => p.dept);
 * // Map {
 * //   'eng' => [{ name: 'Ada', … }, { name: 'Grace', … }],
 * //   'ops' => [{ name: 'Linus', … }],
 * // }
 *
 * @example
 * // Keys can be any type, not just strings:
 * groupBy([1.1, 1.9, 2.3], Math.floor);
 * // Map { 1 => [1.1, 1.9], 2 => [2.3] }
 */
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  const groups = new Map();
  let index = 0;

  for (const item of arr) {
    const key = keyFn(item, index++);
    const bucket = groups.get(key);

    if (bucket === undefined) {
      groups.set(key, [item]);
    } else {
      bucket.push(item);
    }
  }

  return groups;
}
```

### Notes on the design

- **`Map` over plain object.** A plain-object version is a one-line change (`const groups = Object.create(null)`), but you lose non-string keys and gain a footgun around `__proto__`. If you specifically need a plain object, convert at the call site:

  ```js
  const asObject = Object.fromEntries(groupBy(items, keyFn));
  ```

- **`bucket === undefined` instead of `if (bucket)`.** Both work here since buckets are always arrays, but the explicit check makes the intent obvious and stays correct if the bucket type ever changes.

- **`index` is passed to `keyFn`.** It costs nothing and lets callers group by position (e.g. `(_, i) => i % 3`) without a separate `map`/`entries` dance.

- **Iterables, not just arrays.** Using `for…of` means the function works on `Set`, `Map`, generators, and anything else with `Symbol.iterator`, with no extra code.

- **No mutation of the input.** Items are pushed into fresh arrays; the source iterable is only read.

```js
/**
 * Groups the items of an array into an object keyed by the result of `keyFn`.
 *
 * The returned object has a `null` prototype, so it is safe to use arbitrary
 * keys (including `"__proto__"`, `"constructor"`, etc.) without triggering
 * prototype-pollution surprises. Groups are created in the order their keys
 * are first encountered, and each group preserves the original array order.
 *
 * @template T
 * @param {readonly T[]} arr
 *   The array to group. Not mutated.
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, arr: readonly T[]) => PropertyKey} keyFn
 *   Derives the group key from an item. Receives the item, its index, and the
 *   original array. The returned value is coerced to a property key (so
 *   numbers become strings, symbols stay symbols).
 * @returns {Record<PropertyKey, T[]>}
 *   An object mapping each derived key to the array of items that produced it.
 *   Keys with no matches are simply absent.
 * @throws {TypeError} If `arr` is not an array or `keyFn` is not a function.
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => (n % 2 === 0 ? 'even' : 'odd'));
 * // => { odd: [1, 3, 5], even: [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 * const users = [
 *   { name: 'Ada', role: 'admin' },
 *   { name: 'Bob', role: 'user'  },
 *   { name: 'Cy',  role: 'admin' },
 * ];
 * groupBy(users, u => u.role);
 * // => {
 * //   admin: [{ name: 'Ada', role: 'admin' }, { name: 'Cy', role: 'admin' }],
 * //   user:  [{ name: 'Bob', role: 'user'  }],
 * // }
 *
 * @example
 * // Index-aware grouping (e.g. chunk into pairs by index)
 * groupBy(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], (_, i) => Math.floor(i / 2));
 * // => { 0: ['a', 'b'], 1: ['c', 'd'] }
 */
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: `arr` must be an array');
  }
  if (typeof keyFn !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: `keyFn` must be a function');
  }

  // Null-prototype object: no inherited keys, so `__proto__` etc. are safe.
  const result = Object.create(null);

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i, arr);

    if (Object.hasOwn(result, key)) {
      result[key].push(item);
    } else {
      result[key] = [item];
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

### Notes on the design

- **Why `Object.create(null)`?** A plain `{}` inherits `__proto__`, `toString`, etc. If a `keyFn` returns `"__proto__"`, a naive `if (!result[key]) ...` check would misbehave. Using a null-prototype object (and `Object.hasOwn`) makes every string key safe.
- **Complexity.** Single pass: `O(n)` time, `O(n)` space in the number of items plus distinct keys.
- **Key coercion.** Because the result is an object, non-symbol keys are stringified. If you need to preserve numeric or object identity, return a `Map` instead (or wrap this function to build a `Map`).
- **No mutation.** The input array and its items are never modified.
- **Order.** Insertion order of first occurrence is preserved for both keys and within each group, matching `Array.prototype` iteration semantics.

### Optional `Map` variant

If your keys can be non-string values (objects, `NaN`, etc.) and you want to preserve them exactly, swap the accumulator:

```js
function groupByMap(arr, keyFn) {
  const map = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i, arr);
    const bucket = map.get(key);
    if (bucket) bucket.push(item);
    else map.set(key, [item]);
  }
  return map;
}
```

# `groupBy(arr, keyFn)`

Groups the elements of an array by a key derived from each element. Elements are pushed into per-key buckets in their original order.

```js
/**
 * Groups the elements of an array by a key derived from each element.
 *
 * The returned object maps each distinct key to an array of the items that
 * produced it. Items retain their original relative order within each bucket,
 * and keys appear in the order they were first encountered.
 *
 * @template T
 * @template K
 * @param {Iterable<T>} arr
 *        The iterable to group (arrays, Sets, generators, etc.).
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, source: Iterable<T>) => K} keyFn
 *        Called once per element with the element, its 0-based index, and the
 *        original iterable. Its return value is the group key.
 * @returns {Record<string, T[]>}
 *        A null-prototype object whose own keys are the derived group keys
 *        (coerced to strings, except symbols which are preserved) and whose
 *        values are arrays of matching elements.
 * @throws {TypeError} If `arr` is not iterable or `keyFn` is not a function.
 *
 * @example
 *   groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => (n % 2 ? 'odd' : 'even'));
 *   // → { odd: [1, 3, 5], even: [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 *   const people = [
 *     { name: 'Ada',   role: 'eng' },
 *     { name: 'Grace', role: 'eng' },
 *     { name: 'Alan',  role: 'math' },
 *   ];
 *   groupBy(people, p => p.role);
 *   // → { eng: [{...Ada}, {...Grace}], math: [{...Alan}] }
 */
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  if (arr == null || typeof arr[Symbol.iterator] !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: `arr` must be iterable');
  }
  if (typeof keyFn !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: `keyFn` must be a function');
  }

  // Null-prototype object avoids prototype-pollution surprises
  // (e.g. an item whose key is "__proto__" or "constructor").
  const groups = Object.create(null);

  let i = 0;
  for (const item of arr) {
    const rawKey = keyFn(item, i++, arr);
    // Symbols stay as symbols; everything else is coerced to a string so
    // numeric and object keys behave predictably.
    const key = typeof rawKey === 'symbol' ? rawKey : String(rawKey);

    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(groups, key)) {
      groups[key].push(item);
    } else {
      groups[key] = [item];
    }
  }

  return groups;
}
```

### Design notes

- **Null-prototype result.** Using `Object.create(null)` means keys like `"__proto__"` or `"toString"` won't collide with inherited properties. If you need a normal object, wrap the call: `{ ...groupBy(...) }`.
- **Key coercion.** Keys returned by `keyFn` are converted to strings (except `Symbol`s), matching `Map`-free conventions like Lodash's `_.groupBy`. `null` and `undefined` become `"null"` and `"undefined"`.
- **Order preserved.** Both the order of keys and the order of items within each bucket follow the source iteration order.
- **Iterable, not just array.** Accepting any iterable costs nothing and lets you pass `Set`s, generators, or `NodeList`s.

### If you need non-string keys

Use a `Map` instead of an object — it preserves key identity and type:

```js
function groupByMap(arr, keyFn) {
  const groups = new Map();
  let i = 0;
  for (const item of arr) {
    const key = keyFn(item, i++, arr);
    const bucket = groups.get(key);
    bucket ? bucket.push(item) : groups.set(key, [item]);
  }
  return groups;
}
```

Then convert when you're done: `Object.fromEntries(groupByMap(...))`.

```ts
/**
 * Groups the items of an array by a key derived from each item.
 *
 * The returned object maps every derived key to an array of the items that
 * produced it, in their original order. The order of the keys themselves
 * follows the order in which each key was first encountered.
 *
 * Because the result is a plain object, keys are coerced to strings
 * (standard JS object-key behaviour): `1` and `"1"` land in the same bucket.
 * If you need to distinguish non-string keys (objects, `NaN`, etc.), use a
 * `Map` instead.
 *
 * The returned object has a `null` prototype (`Object.create(null)`), so it
 * is safe to use with arbitrary keys such as `"__proto__"` or `"constructor"`
 * without triggering prototype pollution. Note that this means it has no
 * inherited methods like `hasOwnProperty`.
 *
 * @template T - The type of the array items.
 * @template K - The type of the derived key (must be a valid property key).
 *
 * @param arr - The array to group. `null` or `undefined` yields an empty object.
 * @param keyFn - Derives the grouping key for an item. Receives the item and
 *   its zero-based index.
 *
 * @returns An object whose keys are the derived group keys and whose values
 *   are arrays of the matching items.
 *
 * @example
 * // Simple key derivation
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => (n % 2 === 0 ? 'even' : 'odd'));
 * // => { odd: [1, 3, 5], even: [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Grouping objects by a property
 * const people = [
 *   { name: 'Ada',  dept: 'Eng'   },
 *   { name: 'Bob',  dept: 'Sales' },
 *   { name: 'Cleo', dept: 'Eng'   },
 * ];
 * groupBy(people, p => p.dept);
 * // => { Eng: [Ada, Cleo], Sales: [Bob] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Using the index in the key function
 * groupBy(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], (_, i) => (i < 2 ? 'first' : 'rest'));
 * // => { first: ['a', 'b'], rest: ['c', 'd'] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Safe with null/undefined input
 * groupBy(null, String); // => {}
 */
export function groupBy<T, K extends PropertyKey>(
  arr: readonly T[] | null | undefined,
  keyFn: (item: T, index: number) => K,
): Record<K, T[]> {
  // Null-prototype object: no inherited keys, immune to `__proto__` tricks.
  const result = Object.create(null) as Record<K, T[]>;

  if (arr == null) return result;

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i] as T;
    const key = keyFn(item, i);
    const bucket = result[key];

    if (bucket === undefined) {
      result[key] = [item];
    } else {
      bucket.push(item);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

### Design notes

- **Null-prototype result.** Keys often come from user data, so using `Object.create(null)` avoids the classic `__proto__` / `constructor` footgun. The trade-off is that the result has no inherited methods — call `Object.keys(result)` or `Object.hasOwn(result, k)` instead of `result.hasOwnProperty(k)`.
- **String coercion.** This is a plain-object API, so `1` and `"1"` collide. If you need true key identity (including object keys), swap the accumulator for a `Map`:

  ```ts
  export function groupByMap<T, K>(arr: readonly T[], keyFn: (item: T, i: number) => K): Map<K, T[]> {
    const result = new Map<K, T[]>();
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
      const item = arr[i] as T;
      const key = keyFn(item, i);
      const bucket = result.get(key);
      bucket ? bucket.push(item) : result.set(key, [item]);
    }
    return result;
  }
  ```

- **Single pass, O(n).** Insertion order is preserved for both keys and items, matching `Map` semantics.
- **`null`/`undefined` tolerated.** Returning `{}` for missing input keeps callers from having to guard at every call site.

# `groupBy` Utility Function

A small, dependency-free utility that groups array items by a derived key. Keys can be any value that can be used as a `Map` key (strings, numbers, symbols, objects, etc.) — which is more flexible than the classic "object with string keys" approach.

```js
/**
 * Groups the items of an array by a key derived from each item.
 *
 * @template T, K
 * @param {readonly T[]} arr
 *   The array to group. May be empty; may contain duplicates; the input
 *   is never mutated.
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, arr: readonly T[]) => K} keyFn
 *   A function invoked once per item. It receives the item, its index,
 *   and the original array, and must return the key to group by.
 *   Keys are compared with `SameValueZero` semantics (like `Map`), so
 *   strings, numbers, `NaN`, symbols, and object references all work.
 * @returns {Map<K, T[]>}
 *   A `Map` from each distinct key to an array of items that produced it.
 *   Insertion order of keys matches first-seen order; items within each
 *   group preserve their original relative order.
 *
 * @example
 * // Basic usage with a property-derived key
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => (n % 2 === 0 ? 'even' : 'odd'));
 * // => Map { 'odd' => [1, 3, 5], 'even' => [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Grouping objects by a field
 * const people = [
 *   { name: 'Alice', dept: 'Eng' },
 *   { name: 'Bob',   dept: 'Sales' },
 *   { name: 'Carol', dept: 'Eng' },
 * ];
 * groupBy(people, p => p.dept);
 * // => Map {
 * //      'Eng'   => [{ name: 'Alice', ... }, { name: 'Carol', ... }],
 * //      'Sales' => [{ name: 'Bob', ... }],
 * //    }
 *
 * @example
 * // Using the index for round-robin partitioning
 * groupBy(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], (_, i) => i % 2);
 * // => Map { 0 => ['a', 'c'], 1 => ['b', 'd'] }
 */
export function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: `arr` must be an array');
  }
  if (typeof keyFn !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: `keyFn` must be a function');
  }

  /** @type {Map<K, T[]>} */
  const groups = new Map();

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i, arr);

    const bucket = groups.get(key);
    if (bucket === undefined) {
      // First time we see this key — start a new bucket.
      groups.set(key, [item]);
    } else {
      // `Map.get` can't distinguish "missing" from "stored undefined",
      // but we never store `undefined` as a bucket, so this is safe.
      bucket.push(item);
    }
  }

  return groups;
}
```

## Design notes

**Why a `Map` and not a plain object?**

- Object keys are coerced to strings, so `groupBy([1, '1'], String)` would incorrectly merge `1` and `'1'`.
- Keys like `__proto__`, `constructor`, or `hasOwnProperty` can collide with object internals.
- `Map` supports any key type (numbers, symbols, objects) and preserves insertion order.

**Why check `bucket === undefined` instead of `groups.has(key)`?**

- It's a single hash lookup instead of two. It's correct here because we never insert `undefined` as a bucket value (we always insert an array).

**Return type**

The result is a `Map`. If you need a plain object (e.g. for JSON serialization), convert it:

```js
const obj = Object.fromEntries(groupBy(items, fn));
```

**Performance**

- Single pass, `O(n)` time and `O(n)` space.
- `keyFn` is invoked exactly once per item.

**Non-mutation**

The input array and its items are never modified. The returned arrays are fresh.

## Quick tests

```js
// Empty input
groupBy([], x => x); // => Map {}

// Single group
groupBy([1, 1, 1], x => 'a'); // => Map { 'a' => [1, 1, 1] }

// Duplicate items are preserved, not deduped
groupBy([1, 1, 2], x => x % 2); // => Map { 1 => [1, 1], 0 => [2] }

// Object keys use reference identity
const a = {}, b = {};
groupBy([1, 2, 3], (_, i) => (i < 2 ? a : b)); // Map { a => [1,2], b => [3] }
```

# `groupBy(arr, keyFn)`

A small utility that buckets array items into groups based on a key derived from each item.

```js
/**
 * Groups the items of an array into buckets keyed by a value derived from each item.
 *
 * For every element in `arr`, `keyFn` is called with the element (and its index)
 * to produce a key. All elements that produce the same key are collected into
 * an array, and those arrays are stored as properties on a plain object.
 *
 * @template T
 * @param {readonly T[]} arr
 *   The array to group. May be empty. `null`/`undefined` are treated as empty.
 * @param {(item: T, index: number) => string | number | symbol} keyFn
 *   A function that receives each item and its index, and returns the key
 *   used to bucket it. Returned keys are coerced to strings (except symbols)
 *   by normal object property semantics.
 * @returns {Record<string | symbol, T[]>}
 *   A plain object whose own enumerable properties are the distinct keys
 *   produced by `keyFn`, each mapped to an array of the items that produced it.
 *   Insertion order of keys reflects first-seen order. The returned object has
 *   a null prototype so that keys like `"__proto__"` or `"constructor"` are
 *   handled safely.
 *
 * @example
 * // Group numbers by parity
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => (n % 2 === 0 ? 'even' : 'odd'));
 * // => { odd: [1, 3, 5], even: [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Group records by a field
 * const people = [
 *   { name: 'Ada',   team: 'eng' },
 *   { name: 'Grace', team: 'eng' },
 *   { name: 'Linus', team: 'ops' },
 * ];
 * groupBy(people, p => p.team);
 * // => { eng: [{name:'Ada',...}, {name:'Grace',...}], ops: [{name:'Linus',...}] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Use the index to bucket by position
 * groupBy(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], (_item, i) => (i < 2 ? 'first' : 'second'));
 * // => { first: ['a', 'b'], second: ['c', 'd'] }
 */
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  // Null-prototype object avoids surprises with keys like "__proto__".
  const result = Object.create(null);

  if (arr == null) return result;

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i);

    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(result, key)) {
      result[key].push(item);
    } else {
      result[key] = [item];
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

## Design notes

**Why a null-prototype object?** If `keyFn` returns `"__proto__"`, `"constructor"`, or `"toString"`, a normal `{}` would either silently fail or collide with inherited properties. `Object.create(null)` sidesteps this entirely. The trade-off is that the result has no `hasOwnProperty` method — callers who need it should use `Object.hasOwn(result, key)`.

**Why not a `Map`?** A `Map` is arguably the more correct choice when keys may be non-string (e.g. objects). This version mirrors lodash's `groupBy` and the common `Object.groupBy` proposal, which coerce keys to strings. If you need identity-based keys, swap the accumulator for a `Map` — the rest of the logic is identical.

**Why `hasOwnProperty.call` instead of `result[key]` truthiness?** Because a bucket could legitimately be an empty array, and `if (result[key])` would treat it as missing.

**Index parameter.** Passing the index to `keyFn` costs nothing and lets callers bucket by position without first calling `.map((x, i) => ...)`.

## Quick tests

```js
console.assert(JSON.stringify(groupBy([1,2,3,4], n => n % 2 ? 'odd' : 'even'))
  === '{"odd":[1,3],"even":[2,4]}');

console.assert(Object.getPrototypeOf(groupBy([], x => x)) === null);

// Dangerous keys are safe:
const g = groupBy([{k:'__proto__'}], x => x.k);
console.assert(Array.isArray(g['__proto__']) && g['__proto__'].length === 1);

// Empty / nullish input:
console.assert(Object.keys(groupBy(null, x => x)).length === 0);
```

## When you don't need a helper

Modern runtimes ship `Object.groupBy` (ES2024) with the same semantics:

```js
Object.groupBy([1,2,3,4], n => n % 2 ? 'odd' : 'even');
// => { odd: [1,3], even: [2,4] }
```

It returns a null-prototype object too, so the helper above is mainly useful for older environments or if you want the extra `index` argument (which `Object.groupBy` does not pass).

```js
/**
 * Groups the elements of an array by a key derived from each element.
 *
 * The order of the keys in the returned object reflects the order in which
 * each key was first encountered, and the order of the elements within each
 * group reflects their original order in `arr`.
 *
 * @template T
 * @param {T[]} arr
 *   The array to group. Must be a real array (not array-like).
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, array: T[]) => string | number | symbol} keyFn
 *   Callback invoked once per element. It receives the element, its index,
 *   and the original array, and must return the key to group it under.
 *   Keys are coerced to strings by `Object.fromEntries` (except symbols).
 * @returns {Record<string, T[]>}
 *   A plain object whose own keys are the derived group keys and whose
 *   values are arrays of the elements that produced each key. Returns an
 *   empty object for an empty input array.
 * @throws {TypeError} If `arr` is not an array or `keyFn` is not a function.
 *
 * @example
 * // Group by a string property
 * groupBy(
 *   [{ type: 'fruit', name: 'apple' }, { type: 'veg', name: 'carrot' }, { type: 'fruit', name: 'pear' }],
 *   (x) => x.type
 * );
 * // => { fruit: [{ type: 'fruit', name: 'apple' }, { type: 'fruit', name: 'pear' }],
 * //      veg:   [{ type: 'veg', name: 'carrot' }] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Group by a derived key using the index
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], (n, i) => (i % 2 === 0 ? 'even-index' : 'odd-index'));
 * // => { 'even-index': [1, 3, 5], 'odd-index': [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Empty input
 * groupBy([], (x) => x); // => {}
 *
 * @see {@link https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/groupBy | Object.groupBy} (ES2024 native equivalent)
 */
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: first argument must be an array');
  }
  if (typeof keyFn !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: second argument must be a function');
  }

  // A Map is used internally so that keys like "__proto__" or "constructor"
  // cannot collide with Object.prototype members. It also preserves insertion
  // order for both keys and elements.
  const groups = new Map();

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const key = keyFn(arr[i], i, arr);
    const bucket = groups.get(key);
    if (bucket === undefined) {
      groups.set(key, [arr[i]]);
    } else {
      bucket.push(arr[i]);
    }
  }

  // Object.fromEntries defines own properties (it does not invoke the
  // `__proto__` setter), so the result is safe against prototype pollution.
  return Object.fromEntries(groups);
}
```

### Notes on the design

- **Why a `Map` internally?** Using a plain object as the accumulator would let user keys like `"__proto__"` or `"hasOwnProperty"` interfere with `Object.prototype`. A `Map` sidesteps that entirely and preserves insertion order.
- **Why `Object.fromEntries`?** It creates own data properties directly (via `CreateDataPropertyOrThrow`), so even a `"__proto__"` key becomes a normal own property rather than mutating the prototype.
- **Insertion order:** Keys appear in the order they were first produced by `keyFn`; elements within a bucket keep their original relative order.
- **Native alternative:** In ES2024+ environments, `Object.groupBy(arr, keyFn)` does the same thing (except it returns a null-prototype object). This utility is useful for older runtimes or when you want a plain `Object.prototype`-backed result.

```js
/**
 * Groups the elements of an array by a key derived from each element.
 *
 * @template T
 * @template {PropertyKey} K
 * @param {readonly T[]} arr - The array to group. It is not mutated.
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, array: readonly T[]) => K} keyFn -
 *   Function called for each item. Its return value is used as the group key.
 * @returns {Record<K, T[]>} An object whose keys are the derived group keys
 *   and whose values are arrays of items belonging to that group.
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => (n % 2 === 0 ? 'even' : 'odd'));
 * // { odd: [1, 3, 5], even: [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 * const users = [
 *   { id: 1, role: 'admin' },
 *   { id: 2, role: 'user' },
 *   { id: 3, role: 'admin' },
 * ];
 *
 * groupBy(users, user => user.role);
 * // {
 * //   admin: [{ id: 1, role: 'admin' }, { id: 3, role: 'admin' }],
 * //   user: [{ id: 2, role: 'user' }]
 * // }
 *
 * @remarks
 * - Keys are coerced to JavaScript property keys: strings or symbols.
 *   If `keyFn` returns an object, all such items will share the key
 *   "[object Object]". Use a Map-based variant if you need arbitrary keys.
 * - The returned object has a null prototype to avoid prototype pollution,
 *   e.g. when a derived key is "__proto__".
 * - Time complexity: O(n). Space complexity: O(n).
 */
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  const groups = Object.create(null);

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i, arr);

    if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(groups, key)) {
      groups[key] = [];
    }

    groups[key].push(item);
  }

  return groups;
}
```

Example usage:

```js
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

const grouped = groupBy(numbers, n => (n % 2 === 0 ? 'even' : 'odd'));

console.log(grouped);
// { odd: [1, 3, 5], even: [2, 4, 6] }
```

If you need to support arbitrary key values, such as objects or arrays, use a `Map` instead of a plain object:

```js
/**
 * Same as groupBy, but returns a Map so keys can be any value.
 *
 * @template T
 * @template K
 * @param {readonly T[]} arr
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, array: readonly T[]) => K} keyFn
 * @returns {Map<K, T[]>}
 */
function groupByMap(arr, keyFn) {
  const groups = new Map();

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i, arr);

    if (!groups.has(key)) {
      groups.set(key, []);
    }

    groups.get(key).push(item);
  }

  return groups;
}
```

# `groupBy` Utility Function

A small, dependency-free helper that groups array items into an object keyed by the result of a `keyFn`.

```js
/**
 * Groups the items of an array into an object, using a derived key for each item.
 *
 * For every element in `arr`, `keyFn` is called with `(item, index, arr)` and its
 * return value is used as the property name under which the item is collected.
 * Items that produce the same key are pushed into the same array, preserving
 * their relative order from the input.
 *
 * Notes:
 *  - Keys are coerced to strings (JavaScript object property semantics), so
 *    `1` and `'1'` collide. Use a `Map` if you need distinct non-string keys.
 *  - Symbol keys are supported via `Object.defineProperty`.
 *  - The result is a plain object with a `null` prototype to avoid prototype
 *    pollution (e.g. a key of `"__proto__"` won't break anything).
 *  - This function does not mutate `arr`.
 *
 * @template T
 * @param {ArrayLike<T>} arr - The collection to group. Must be array-like
 *   (has a numeric `length` and indexed access).
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, arr: ArrayLike<T>) => PropertyKey} keyFn
 *   Function that returns the group key for a given item.
 * @returns {Record<string, T[]>} An object mapping each key to the array of
 *   items that produced it. Keys with no items are simply absent.
 * @throws {TypeError} If `arr` is null/undefined or `keyFn` is not a function.
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => (n % 2 ? 'odd' : 'even'));
 * // => { odd: [1, 3, 5], even: [2, 4] }
 *
 * @example
 * const people = [
 *   { name: 'Ada',   dept: 'eng' },
 *   { name: 'Linus', dept: 'eng' },
 *   { name: 'Grace', dept: 'ops' },
 * ];
 * groupBy(people, p => p.dept);
 * // => { eng: [ {name:'Ada',...}, {name:'Linus',...} ],
 * //      ops: [ {name:'Grace',...} ] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Key function receives the index too:
 * groupBy(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], (_, i) => (i < 2 ? 'first' : 'second'));
 * // => { first: ['a','b'], second: ['c','d'] }
 */
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  if (arr == null) {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: arr must not be null or undefined');
  }
  if (typeof keyFn !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: keyFn must be a function');
  }

  // Null-prototype object avoids surprises with keys like "__proto__",
  // "constructor", "hasOwnProperty", etc.
  const result = Object.create(null);

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i, arr);

    if (typeof key === 'symbol') {
      // Symbols aren't valid string property names; define them explicitly.
      if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(result, key)) {
        Object.defineProperty(result, key, {
          value: [],
          enumerable: true,
          writable: true,
          configurable: true,
        });
      }
      result[key].push(item);
    } else {
      const k = String(key);
      // `in` works here because the prototype is null.
      if (!(k in result)) result[k] = [];
      result[k].push(item);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

## Design choices worth calling out

| Decision | Why |
|---|---|
| Uses `Object.create(null)` | Prevents prototype pollution and avoids `hasOwnProperty` guards. Keys like `"__proto__"` are treated as ordinary strings. |
| Returns `Record<string, T[]>` not a `Map` | Matches the common "Lodash-style" contract and works well for JSON serialization. Swap to `Map` if you need to preserve non-string key identity (e.g. objects, numbers vs. strings). |
| Iterates with an index-based `for` loop | Works with array-likes (e.g. `arguments`, `NodeList`) and avoids allocating an iterator. |
| Passes `(item, index, arr)` to `keyFn` | Mirrors `Array.prototype.map` and friends, so callers can derive keys from position when needed. |
| Throws on bad input | Fail-fast is friendlier than silently returning `{}`. |
| Does not mutate `arr` | Predictable, side-effect-free. |

## Variations you might want

**Return a `Map` instead** — preserves key identity (numbers, objects, `NaN`):

```js
function groupByMap(arr, keyFn) {
  const m = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i, arr);
    const bucket = m.get(key);
    if (bucket) bucket.push(item);
    else m.set(key, [item]);
  }
  return m;
}
```

**Reduce-style one-liner** (less readable, no null-prototype safety):

```js
const groupBy = (arr, keyFn) =>
  arr.reduce((acc, item, i) => {
    const k = keyFn(item, i, arr);
    (acc[k] ??= []).push(item);
    return acc;
  }, {});
```

## Tests to keep you honest

```js
// Basic grouping
groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4], n => (n % 2 ? 'odd' : 'even'));
// { odd: [1, 3], even: [2, 4] }

// Empty input
groupBy([], x => x);              // {}

// Prototype-pollution safety
groupBy(['x'], () => '__proto__');
// Object with a real "__proto__" own-property (not the global Object.prototype)

// Index-aware keyFn
