### API Overview and Usage

The class keeps things simple: it stores values in a `Map`, supports an optional TTL, and lazily removes expired entries when you access them.

- **`get(key)`** returns the stored value or `undefined`. If the entry has expired, it deletes it first.
- **`set(key, value, ttl?)`** stores or overwrites a value. You can pass a per-entry TTL in milliseconds, or `null` to make it never expire. Returns the cache instance for chaining.
- **`has(key)`** checks whether a live entry exists, and also cleans up expired entries as a side effect.
- **`delete(key)`** removes an entry and returns `true` if it existed.
- **`clear()`** and **`size`** are included as small conveniences: they let you wipe the cache or inspect how many entries are currently stored (including expired ones not yet evicted).

The constructor accepts an optional `ttl` option, which becomes the default lifetime for entries that don't specify their own. Expiration is lazy, so expired entries may remain in memory until `get` or `has` touches them.
---

**Optimization Tip:** You can adjust the default TTL by passing `{ ttl: 5000 }` to the constructor, or override it per entry with the third argument of `set`. If you need proactive cleanup instead of lazy eviction, consider adding a periodic sweep.

Here's a small in-memory cache with lazy TTL expiry. TypeScript, but the JSDoc applies equally if you strip the types.

```ts
/** A stored value plus its expiry metadata. */
interface Entry<V> {
  value: V;
  /** Epoch milliseconds at which the entry expires, or `null` for never. */
  expiresAt: number | null;
}

/**
 * A small in-memory key-value cache with optional per-entry TTLs.
 *
 * Expiry is lazy: an expired entry is discarded the next time it is touched by
 * `get`, `has`, or `delete`. There is no background timer and no size limit, so
 * memory is only reclaimed for keys that are looked up again.
 *
 * @typeParam K - Key type.
 * @typeParam V - Value type.
 */
export class Cache<K, V> {
  private readonly entries = new Map<K, Entry<V>>();

  /**
   * Returns the live value stored under `key`.
   *
   * @param key - Key to look up.
   * @returns The stored value, or `undefined` if the key is missing or expired.
   *   Use {@link has} to distinguish "absent" from "present and `undefined`".
   */
  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    return this.liveEntry(key)?.value;
  }

  /**
   * Stores `value` under `key`, replacing any existing entry.
   *
   * @param key - Key to store under.
   * @param value - Value to store.
   * @param ttlMs - Optional time-to-live in milliseconds. Omit for an entry
   *   that never expires.
   * @returns This cache, so calls can be chained.
   * @throws {RangeError} If `ttlMs` is supplied but is not a positive finite number.
   */
  set(key: K, value: V, ttlMs?: number): this {
    if (ttlMs !== undefined && (!Number.isFinite(ttlMs) || ttlMs <= 0)) {
      throw new RangeError('ttlMs must be a positive finite number');
    }
    this.entries.set(key, {
      value,
      expiresAt: ttlMs === undefined ? null : Date.now() + ttlMs,
    });
    return this;
  }

  /**
   * Reports whether a live entry exists for `key`.
   *
   * @param key - Key to check.
   * @returns `true` if a non-expired entry is present.
   */
  has(key: K): boolean {
    return this.liveEntry(key) !== undefined;
  }

  /**
   * Removes the entry stored under `key`.
   *
   * @param key - Key to remove.
   * @returns `true` if a live entry was removed, `false` if the key was missing
   *   or already expired.
   */
  delete(key: K): boolean {
    if (this.liveEntry(key) === undefined) return false;
    return this.entries.delete(key);
  }

  /** Returns the entry for `key` if it exists and has not expired. */
  private liveEntry(key: K): Entry<V> | undefined {
    const entry = this.entries.get(key);
    if (entry === undefined) return undefined;
    if (entry.expiresAt !== null && entry.expiresAt <= Date.now()) {
      this.entries.delete(key);
      return undefined;
    }
    return entry;
  }
}
