## `groupBy`

```js
/**
 * Groups the items of an array by a key derived from each item.
 *
 * The key for each item is computed by `keyFn`. Items that produce the same
 * key (compared with `SameValueZero`, i.e. `Map` semantics) are collected
 * into a single bucket, in their original relative order.
 *
 * @template T  - The element type of the input array.
 * @template K  - The type of the derived key.
 *
 * @param {readonly T[]} arr
 *   The array to group. Must be an actual `Array` (not an array-like).
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, arr: readonly T[]) => K} keyFn
 *   Derives the grouping key for an item. Receives the item, its index, and
 *   the source array. May return any value, including objects and symbols.
 *
 * @returns {Map<K, T[]>}
 *   A map from key to the array of items with that key. Keys appear in the
 *   order they were first encountered; items within a bucket keep their
 *   original order. The map is empty if `arr` is empty.
 *
 * @throws {TypeError}
 *   If `arr` is not an array or `keyFn` is not a function.
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy([1.2, 2.3, 1.1], n => Math.floor(n));
 * // Map { 1 => [1.2, 1.1], 2 => [2.3] }
 *
 * @example
 * const users = [
 *   { name: 'Ada',  role: 'admin' },
 *   { name: 'Alan', role: 'user'  },
 *   { name: 'Grace', role: 'admin' },
 * ];
 * groupBy(users, u => u.role);
 * // Map { 'admin' => [Ada, Grace], 'user' => [Alan] }
 *
 * @example
 * // Keys may be objects — they are compared by reference.
 * const byIdentity = groupBy(users, u => u);
 * byIdentity.get(users[0]); // [users[0]]
 */
export function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: expected `arr` to be an array');
  }
  if (typeof keyFn !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: expected `keyFn` to be a function');
  }

  /** @type {Map<K, T[]>} */
  const groups = new Map();

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const key = keyFn(item, i, arr);

    const bucket = groups.get(key);
    if (bucket === undefined) {
      // First time we see this key: start a new bucket.
      groups.set(key, [item]);
    } else {
      bucket.push(item);
    }
  }

  return groups;
}
```

### Why a `Map` and not a plain object?

- **Key types are preserved.** `groupBy(arr, n => n)` gives you numeric keys; a plain object would stringify them to `"1"`, `"2"`.
- **No collisions with `Object.prototype`.** A plain object would treat the key `"toString"` or `"__proto__"` as inherited properties, silently breaking the grouping.
- **Any key works.** Objects, symbols, `null`, and `undefined` are all valid keys.

### Companion: `groupByToObject`

If you specifically need a plain object (e.g. for JSON serialization), use a null-prototype object so that odd keys can't collide with built-ins:

```js
/**
 * Like {@link groupBy}, but returns a plain object whose keys are the
 * stringified grouping keys.
 *
 * @template T
 * @param {readonly T[]} arr
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, arr: readonly T[]) => string|number|symbol} keyFn
 * @returns {Record<string, T[]>}
 */
export function groupByToObject(arr, keyFn) {
  /** @type {Record<string, T[]>} */
  const result = Object.create(null);

  for (const [key, items] of groupBy(arr, keyFn)) {
    // Symbols can't be object keys, so they are dropped here.
    if (typeof key === 'symbol') continue;
    result[String(key)] = items;
  }

  return result;
}
```

### Notes & edge cases

| Situation | Behavior |
|---|---|
| `arr` is empty | Returns an empty `Map` (no call to `keyFn`). |
| `keyFn` returns `undefined`/`null` | Treated as a normal key; `undefined` and `null` are distinct keys. |
| `keyFn` returns `NaN` | All `NaN`s group together (`SameValueZero`). |
| `keyFn` returns an object | Grouped by reference identity, not by structure. |
| `keyFn` throws | The exception propagates; nothing is returned. |
| `arr` is mutated by `keyFn` | The loop reads `arr.length` each iteration, so appended items are also visited. Avoid mutating the input. |

**Complexity:** O(n) time and O(n) space, with one `keyFn` call per element.

