# `groupBy`

A small, dependency-free utility that buckets array items by a key you derive from each item.

```js
/**
 * Groups the items of an array (or any iterable) by a key derived from each item.
 *
 * Keys are compared with SameValueZero, so `NaN` is a valid key and `0` and `-0`
 * are treated as the same key. Key *identity* is preserved: two different object
 * keys never collide, even if they look alike.
 *
 * @template T
 * @param {Iterable<T>} arr        The items to group. `null`/`undefined` throws.
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, source: Iterable<T>) => unknown} keyFn
 *        Derives the group key for an item. Called exactly once per item.
 * @returns {Map<unknown, T[]>}
 *        A Map from key -> array of matching items, in first-seen key order.
 *        Items within a group keep their original relative order.
 *
 * @throws {TypeError} If `arr` is not iterable or `keyFn` is not a function.
 *
 * @example
 * groupBy(['one', 'two', 'three'], s => s.length);
 * // Map(3) { 3 => ['one', 'two', 'three'], 5 => [] } -- see note below
 *
 * @example
 * const users = [
 *   { name: 'Ada',  role: 'admin' },
 *   { name: 'Alan', role: 'user'  },
 *   { name: 'Grace', role: 'admin' },
 * ];
 * groupBy(users, u => u.role);
 * // Map(2) {
 * //   'admin' => [{ name: 'Ada', role: 'admin' }, { name: 'Grace', role: 'admin' }],
 * //   'user'  => [{ name: 'Alan', role: 'user' }]
 * // }
 */
export function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  if (arr == null || typeof arr[Symbol.iterator] !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: expected an iterable, received ' + describe(arr));
  }
  if (typeof keyFn !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError('groupBy: expected keyFn to be a function, received ' + describe(keyFn));
  }

  /** @type {Map<unknown, T[]>} */
  const groups = new Map();
  let index = 0;

  // Fast path for arrays: indexed access avoids iterator overhead and lets us
  // pass the live index to keyFn.
  if (Array.isArray(arr)) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
      add(groups, arr[i], keyFn(arr[i], i, arr));
    }
  } else {
    for (const item of arr) {
      add(groups, item, keyFn(item, index++, arr));
    }
  }

  return groups;
}

/** Appends `item` to the bucket for `key`, creating the bucket if needed. */
function add(groups, item, key) {
  const bucket = groups.get(key);
  if (bucket === undefined) {
    groups.set(key, [item]);
  } else {
    bucket.push(item);
  }
}

function describe(value) {
  if (value === null) return 'null';
  if (typeof value === 'object') return Object.prototype.toString.call(value);
  return typeof value;
}
```

---

## Why a `Map` and not a plain object?

| | `Map` (this impl) | Plain object |
|---|---|---|
| Key types | Any value: objects, `Symbol`s, `undefined`, `NaN` | Strings/numbers only (others coerce) |
| Key identity | Object keys stay distinct by reference | Coerced to the same string → collisions |
| Lookup cost | O(1) average | O(1) average |
| Ordering | Insertion order | Integer-like keys sort first — surprising |
| Safety | No `__proto__` / `constructor` pitfalls | Prototype-pollution risk if keys are untrusted |

If you specifically need a JSON-serializable result, use the companion below — it builds the object with `Object.create(null)` so untrusted keys can't reach `Object.prototype`.

```js
/**
 * Like {@link groupBy}, but returns a null-prototype plain object.
 * Keys are stringified with `String(key)`; `undefined` becomes `"undefined"`.
 *
 * @template T
 * @param {Iterable<T>} arr
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, source: Iterable<T>) => unknown} keyFn
 * @returns {Record<string, T[]>}
 */
export function groupByToObject(arr, keyFn) {
  /** @type {Record<string, T[]>} */
  const out = Object.create(null);
  for (const [key, items] of groupBy(arr, keyFn)) {
    const k = String(key);
    if (k in out) out[k].push(...items);
    else out[k] = items;
  }
  return out;
}
```

---

## Usage

```js
const words = ['apple', 'avocado', 'banana', 'blueberry', 'cherry'];

// Group by first letter
const byLetter = groupBy(words, w => w[0]);
byLetter.get('a'); // ['apple', 'avocado']

// Group by a computed property
const orders = [
  { id: 1, total: 120, currency: 'USD' },
  { id: 2, total:  80, currency: 'EUR' },
  { id: 3, total:  200, currency: 'USD' },
];
const byCurrency = groupBy(orders, o => o.currency);
byCurrency.get('USD').length; // 2

// keyFn receives (item, index, source) — handy for round-robins
const pairs = groupBy(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], (_, i) => i % 2);
pairs.get(0); // ['a', 'c']
pairs.get(1); // ['b', 'd']

// Works with any iterable
groupBy(new Set([1, 2, 3]), n => n % 2 ? 'odd' : 'even'); // Map { 'odd' => [1, 3], 'even' => [2] }
```

---

## Behaviour notes & edge cases

- **Empty input** → returns an empty `Map`; no error.
- **`keyFn` returning `undefined` or `null`** → that becomes a normal group key (`map.get(undefined)`).
- **`keyFn` returning `NaN`** → all `NaN` keys land in the same group (SameValueZero).
- **Duplicate items** are all retained; nothing is deduplicated.
- **`keyFn` is called exactly once per item**, so it may have side effects — don't rely on it being called again.
- **Mutating `arr` during iteration**: for arrays the loop reads `arr.length` each tick, so items appended mid-loop are picked up (like a `for` loop). For other iterables, the iterator's own rules apply.
- **Throwing `keyFn`** propagates out of `groupBy` unchanged; partial groups are discarded.

## Complexity

- **Time:** O(n) — one pass, one `keyFn` call per item, amortized O(1) map operations.
- **Space:** O(n) — every item is stored exactly once, plus one array per distinct key.

