## Design notes & caveats

**Why non-2xx responses are returned rather than thrown.** `fetch` only rejects on network-level failure. Keeping that contract means the caller writes one error path (`!res.ok`) instead of two, and the retry logic never has to guess whether a `404` should be re-requested.

**Why `POST` isn't retried by default.** Replaying a `POST` can double-charge a card or send a duplicate email. The retry budget is spent only where a replay is safe unless you opt in with `retryNonIdempotent: true`.

**Why jitter.** Without it, every client that failed during an outage wakes up simultaneously and re-creates the overload. Equal jitter keeps a guaranteed floor (so we don't spin) while spreading the load.

**Timeouts are per attempt, not total.** `timeout: 2_000` with `retries: 3` means up to ~8 s of wall clock plus backoff. If you need a hard total deadline, use an `AbortSignal` with `AbortSignal.timeout(ms)` — the abort propagates immediately and cancels any pending backoff sleep.

**`Retry-After` wins over the computed backoff**, but is clamped to `maxDelay` so a hostile or buggy server can't stall the caller for an hour.

**CommonJS:** replace `export async function fetchWithRetry` with `async function fetchWithRetry` and add `module.exports = { fetchWithRetry };`.

**Possible extensions:** a `retryAfter` cap separate from `maxDelay`, a circuit breaker in front of this helper, or a `Retry-After` budget shared across a batch of requests.

## `fetchWithRetry`

A dependency-free implementation for Node.js 18+ / modern browsers, using the built-in `fetch`.

```js
/**
 * @file fetch-with-retry.js
 */

/**
 * HTTP status codes that are treated as *transient* by default: the server is
 * likely to succeed if the same request is sent again shortly.
 *
 * - 408 Request Timeout
 * - 425 Too Early
 * - 429 Too Many Requests        (honours the `Retry-After` header)
 * - 500 Internal Server Error
 * - 502 Bad Gateway
 * - 503 Service Unavailable      (honours the `Retry-After` header)
 * - 504 Gateway Timeout
 *
 * @type {Set<number>}
 */
const RETRYABLE_STATUS_CODES = new Set([408, 425, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504]);

/** Default retry configuration. */
const DEFAULT_RETRY = Object.freeze({
  /** Base delay in milliseconds for the exponential backoff. */
  baseDelay: 300,
  /** Upper bound (ms) for any single wait between attempts. */
  maxDelay: 30_000,
  /** Apply "full jitter" (randomised delay) to avoid thundering herds. */
  jitter: true,
  /** Per-attempt timeout in ms. `0` disables it. */
  timeout: 0,
  /**
   * Optional allow-list of HTTP methods that may be retried, e.g. `['GET', 'HEAD']`.
   * `undefined` (default) allows every method.
   */
  methods: undefined,
  /** Predicate deciding whether a response status should be retried. */
  retryOn: (status) => RETRYABLE_STATUS_CODES.has(status),
});

/**
 * Fetches a URL, retrying failed requests with exponential backoff.
 *
 * ## Behaviour
 *
 * - The request is attempted up to `retries + 1` times (1 initial attempt + `retries` retries).
 * - A request is retried when it fails with a **network error** (DNS failure, connection reset,
 *   socket hang-up, per-attempt timeout, …) or when the response has a **retryable status**
 *   (see {@link RETRYABLE_STATUS_CODES}).
 * - The wait before the next attempt grows exponentially:
 *   `baseDelay * 2^attempt`, capped at `maxDelay`. With `jitter: true` the delay is
 *   randomised within `[0, exponential]` ("full jitter"), which prevents many clients from
 *   retrying at the same instant.
 * - If the server sends a `Retry-After` header (seconds or an HTTP date), that value is used
 *   instead of — or in addition to — the computed backoff, capped by `maxDelay`.
 * - The **final** response is returned as-is, including non-2xx responses, so the caller can
 *   inspect `response.status` / `response.body`. An exception is thrown only when the request
 *   could not be completed at all (network error after the last attempt) or when the caller
 *   aborts via `options.signal`.
 *
 * ## Aborting
 *
 * Pass an `AbortSignal` in `options.signal` to cancel the whole operation (including any
 * pending backoff wait). Aborting is never retried. A per-attempt timeout can be configured
 * with `retry.timeout`; timeouts *are* retried because they are usually transient.
 *
 * ## Caveats
 *
 * - `options.body` must be replayable across attempts (string, `Buffer`, `Blob`, `FormData`, …).
 *   A consumed stream cannot be re-sent, so streaming bodies cannot be retried.
 * - Retrying non-idempotent requests (e.g. `POST`) may duplicate side effects. Use
 *   `retry.methods` to restrict retries to safe methods, and/or send an idempotency key.
 *
 * @param {string|URL|Request} url            The resource to fetch.
 * @param {object}  [options]                  Standard `fetch` options, plus an optional `retry` config.
 * @param {object}  [options.retry]            Retry configuration; see {@link DEFAULT_RETRY}.
 * @param {number}  [options.retry.baseDelay]  Base backoff delay in ms (default `300`).
 * @param {number}  [options.retry.maxDelay]   Maximum backoff delay in ms (default `30000`).
 * @param {boolean} [options.retry.jitter]     Randomise delays (default `true`).
 * @param {number}  [options.retry.timeout]    Per-attempt timeout in ms (default `0` = none).
 * @param {string[]} [options.retry.methods]   HTTP methods eligible for retry (default: all).
 * @param {(status:number)=>boolean} [options.retry.retryOn] Status predicate (default: transient statuses).
 * @param {AbortSignal} [options.signal]       Signal used to cancel the operation.
 * @param {number}  retries                    Number of retries *after* the first attempt (default `3`).
 *
 * @returns {Promise<Response>} The final `Response` (may be a non-2xx response).
 *
 * @throws {TypeError}   If `retries` is not a non-negative integer.
 * @throws {Error}       If `fetch` is unavailable in the current environment.
 * @throws {Error|unknown} The last network error, if every attempt failed.
 * @throws {AbortError}  If the caller's `AbortSignal` was triggered.
 *
 * @example
 * // Basic usage: up to 3 retries with exponential backoff.
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data');
 * if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
 *
 * @example
 * // Custom backoff, per-attempt timeout, and cancellation.
 * const controller = new AbortController();
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry(
 *   'https://api.example.com/data',
 *   {
 *     method: 'POST',
 *     headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
 *     body: JSON.stringify({ hello: 'world' }),
 *     signal: controller.signal,
 *     retry: { baseDelay: 500, maxDelay: 10_000, timeout: 5_000, methods: ['POST'] },
 *   },
 *   5,
 * );
 */
export async function fetchWithRetry(url, options = {}, retries = 3) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(retries) || retries < 0) {
    throw new TypeError('retries must be a non-negative integer');
  }
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch !== 'function') {
    throw new Error('fetch is not available in this environment (use Node.js 18+ or a browser)');
  }

  const { retry, signal: callerSignal, ...fetchOptions } = options ?? {};
  const config = { ...DEFAULT_RETRY, ...(retry ?? {}) };

  const method = String(
    fetchOptions.method ?? (typeof Request === 'function' && url instanceof Request ? url.method : 'GET'),
  ).toUpperCase();
  const methodAllowed = Array.isArray(config.methods)
    ? config.methods.some((m) => String(m).toUpperCase() === method)
    : true;

  const maxAttempts = methodAllowed ? retries + 1 : 1;
  let lastError;

  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < maxAttempts; attempt += 1) {
    const isLastAttempt = attempt === maxAttempts - 1;
    const timeoutController = config.timeout > 0 ? createTimeoutController(config.timeout) : null;
    const { signal: attemptSignal, cleanup: cleanupSignals } = combineSignals([
      callerSignal,
      timeoutController?.signal,
    ]);

    let waitMs = null;

    try {
      const response = await globalThis.fetch(url, { ...fetchOptions, signal: attemptSignal });

      const retryableStatus = !response.ok && config.retryOn(response.status);
      if (!retryableStatus || isLastAttempt) {
        return response; // Success, or a status the caller should handle itself.
      }

      // Retryable status: prefer the server's `Retry-After`, else exponential backoff.
      const backoff = computeBackoff(attempt, config);
      const retryAfter = parseRetryAfter(response.headers.get('retry-after'));
      waitMs = Math.min(config.maxDelay, Math.max(backoff, retryAfter ?? 0));
      lastError = new Error(`Request failed with status ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`.trim());

      await discardBody(response); // Release the connection; this response is thrown away.
    } catch (error) {
      if (callerSignal?.aborted) throw error; // Caller cancelled: never retry.
      lastError = error;
      if (isLastAttempt) throw error;
      waitMs = computeBackoff(attempt, config);
    } finally {
      cleanupSignals();
      timeoutController?.cleanup();
    }

    await sleep(waitMs, callerSignal);
  }

  /* istanbul ignore next — unreachable: the loop either returns or throws. */
  throw lastError ?? new Error('Request failed');
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ helpers */

/**
 * Computes the exponential backoff delay for a given (0-based) attempt.
 *
 * @param {number} attempt  Number of attempts already made.
 * @param {{baseDelay:number,maxDelay:number,jitter:boolean}} config
 * @returns {number} Delay in milliseconds.
 */
function computeBackoff(attempt, { baseDelay, maxDelay, jitter }) {
  const exponential = Math.min(maxDelay, baseDelay * 2 ** attempt);
  return jitter ? Math.round(Math.random() * exponential) : exponential;
}

/**
 * Parses a `Retry-After` header (delay in seconds or an HTTP date).
 *
 * @param {string|null} value
 * @param {number} [now]
 * @returns {number|null} Delay in milliseconds, or `null` if the header is absent/invalid.
 */
function parseRetryAfter(value, now = Date.now()) {
  if (value == null || value === '') return null;
  const seconds = Number(value);
  if (Number.isFinite(seconds)) return Math.max(0, seconds * 1000);
  const timestamp = Date.parse(value);
  if (!Number.isNaN(timestamp)) return Math.max(0, timestamp - now);
  return null;
}

/**
 * Resolves after `ms` milliseconds, rejecting early if `signal` is aborted.
 *
 * @param {number} ms
 * @param {AbortSignal} [signal]
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
function sleep(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      reject(abortReason(signal));
      return;
    }
    const onAbort = () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(abortReason(signal));
    };
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      signal?.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
      resolve();
    }, ms);
    signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}

/**
 * Creates a controller that aborts after `timeoutMs`.
 *
 * @param {number} timeoutMs
 * @returns {{signal: AbortSignal, cleanup: () => void}}
 */
function createTimeoutController(timeoutMs) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timer = setTimeout(() => {
    controller.abort(createAbortError(`Request timed out after ${timeoutMs} ms`));
  }, timeoutMs);
  timer.unref?.(); // Node.js: don't keep the event loop alive just for the timer.
  return { signal: controller.signal, cleanup: () => clearTimeout(timer) };
}

/**
 * Combines several signals into one.
 *
 * @param {Array<AbortSignal|undefined>} signals
 * @returns {{signal: AbortSignal|undefined, cleanup: () => void}}
 */
function combineSignals(signals) {
  const active = signals.filter(Boolean);
  if (active.length === 0) return { signal: undefined, cleanup: () => {} };
  if (active.length === 1) return { signal: active[0], cleanup: () => {} };

  if (typeof AbortSignal.any === 'function') {
    return { signal: AbortSignal.any(active), cleanup: () => {} };
  }

  const controller = new AbortController();
  const onAbort = (event) => controller.abort(event.target.reason);
  for (const signal of active) {
    if (signal.aborted) {
      controller.abort(signal.reason);
      break;
    }
    signal.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  }
  return {
    signal: controller.signal,
    cleanup: () => active.forEach((signal) => signal.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort)),
  };
}

/**
 * Drains and discards a response body so the connection can be reused.
 *
 * @param {Response} response
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
async function discardBody(response) {
  try {
    if (response.body && typeof response.body.cancel === 'function') {
      await response.body.cancel();
    } else {
      await response.arrayBuffer?.();
    }
  } catch {
    /* Ignore: the response is being discarded anyway. */
  }
}

/**
 * Returns the abort reason as an `AbortError`.
 *
 * @param {AbortSignal} signal
 * @returns {Error}
 */
function abortReason(signal) {
  const reason = signal?.reason;
  if (reason instanceof Error) return reason;
  return createAbortError(typeof reason === 'string' ? reason : undefined);
}

/**
 * Creates an `AbortError`.
 *
 * @param {string} [message]
 * @returns {Error}
 */
function createAbortError(message) {
  const text = message ?? 'The operation was aborted.';
  if (typeof DOMException === 'function') return new DOMException(text, 'AbortError');
  const error = new Error(text);
  error.name = 'AbortError';
  return error;
}
```

### Usage

```js
// 1. Simple: 3 retries, default backoff (300ms → 600ms → 1200ms, jittered).
const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/users');

// 2. Custom policy + per-attempt timeout.
const res = await fetchWithRetry(
  'https://api.example.com/users',
  {
    headers: { authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
    signal: AbortSignal.timeout(60_000), // cancels everything
    retry: { baseDelay: 500, maxDelay: 10_000, timeout: 5_000, jitter: true },
  },
  5,
);

// 3. Only retry safe reads.
const res = await fetchWithRetry(url, { retry: { methods: ['GET', 'HEAD'] } }, 3);
```

### Key design points

| Aspect | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| Total attempts | `retries + 1` |
| Retried on | Network errors, per-attempt timeouts, and transient HTTP statuses (`408, 425, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504`) |
| Not retried on | Caller aborts, non-retryable statuses (e.g. `404`, `401`) — the response is returned for you to handle |
| Backoff | `min(maxDelay, baseDelay · 2^attempt)`, randomised with full jitter |
| `Retry-After` | Honoured for `429`/`503` (seconds or HTTP date), capped by `maxDelay` |
| Return value | The final `Response` (possibly non-2xx); throws only on unrecoverable network failure or abort |

Below is a production‑grade implementation. It uses the global `fetch` (Node 18+, Deno, Bun, or any modern browser), so there are no dependencies.

```js
/* ============================================================================
 * fetchWithRetry — retry a fetch() request with exponential backoff + jitter
 * ==========================================================================*/

/** HTTP statuses that are treated as *transient* and therefore retried. */
const DEFAULT_RETRYABLE_STATUSES = Object.freeze([
  408, // Request Timeout
  425, // Too Early
  429, // Too Many Requests
  500, // Internal Server Error
  502, // Bad Gateway
  503, // Service Unavailable
  504, // Gateway Timeout
]);

/**
 * HTTP methods that are safe to replay: replaying them cannot duplicate a
 * server-side side effect (RFC 9110 §9.2.2).
 */
const IDEMPOTENT_METHODS = Object.freeze([
  'GET', 'HEAD', 'OPTIONS', 'PUT', 'DELETE', 'TRACE',
]);

/**
 * Error thrown when every attempt failed with a retryable HTTP status.
 *
 * The last `Response` is attached (its body is *not* consumed, so you can
 * still read it) together with the number of attempts made.
 */
export class RetryError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {string} message
   * @param {{response?: Response, attempts: number, cause?: unknown}} details
   */
  constructor(message, { response, attempts, cause } = {}) {
    super(message, { cause });
    this.name = 'RetryError';
    this.response = response;
    this.attempts = attempts;
  }
}

/** Error thrown when a single attempt exceeded the configured timeout. */
export class TimeoutError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {number} ms
   * @param {unknown} [cause]
   */
  constructor(ms, cause) {
    super(`Request timed out after ${ms}ms`, { cause });
    this.name = 'TimeoutError';
    this.timeout = ms;
  }
}

/**
 * @typedef {object} RetryConfig
 * @property {number}  [baseDelay=300]      Delay (ms) for the first retry.
 * @property {number}  [maxDelay=30000]     Upper bound (ms) for any single delay.
 * @property {number}  [factor=2]           Exponential growth factor.
 * @property {boolean} [jitter=true]        Randomize the delay to avoid thundering herds.
 * @property {number}  [timeout=10000]      Per-attempt timeout in ms (0 = no timeout).
 * @property {Set<number>|number[]} [retryableStatuses] Statuses to retry.
 * @property {boolean} [idempotentOnly=true] Only replay idempotent methods.
 * @property {boolean} [respectRetryAfter=true] Honour the `Retry-After` header.
 * @property {(info: RetryInfo) => void} [onRetry] Called before each retry.
 * @property {Console|null} [logger=console] Where to log warnings.
 */

/**
 * @typedef {object} RetryInfo
 * @property {number}  attempt   1-based number of the attempt that just failed.
 * @property {number}  delay     Milliseconds we are about to wait.
 * @property {Error}   [error]   The network/timeout error, if any.
 * @property {Response} [response] The response, if the failure was an HTTP status.
 * @property {string}  url
 */

/**
 * Fetch a URL, retrying transient failures with exponential backoff and jitter.
 *
 * ## Behaviour
 *
 * - **Retries** network errors (DNS failure, connection reset, TLS errors) and
 *   transient HTTP statuses (`408, 425, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504` by default).
 * - **Does not retry** other `4xx` client errors — they will not get better on
 *   a second attempt.
 * - **Backoff** is `baseDelay * factor ** attempt`, capped at `maxDelay` and
 *   randomized ("equal jitter") so that many clients do not retry in lockstep.
 * - **`Retry-After`** is honoured for `429`/`503` responses when present.
 * - **Timeouts** are applied per attempt, not to the whole operation.
 * - **Cancellation** via `options.signal` aborts immediately and is never retried.
 *
 * ## Resolving vs. rejecting
 *
 * The promise resolves with the first non-retryable response (including `4xx`
 * and `5xx` responses that are not in the retryable set) — inspect
 * `response.ok` / `response.status` yourself. It rejects only when *no* usable
 * response could be obtained:
 *
 * - `RetryError`  — the server answered, but with a retryable status on every attempt.
 * - `Error`       — the last network error (e.g. `TypeError: fetch failed`).
 * - `TimeoutError`— the last attempt timed out.
 *
 * ## Safety
 *
 * By default only **idempotent** methods (`GET`, `HEAD`, `PUT`, `DELETE`, …) are
 * replayed. Retrying a `POST` can duplicate side effects (double charge, double
 * email). If your endpoint is idempotent (e.g. it carries an idempotency key),
 * set `retry.idempotentOnly = false`.
 *
 * @param {string|URL|Request} url                       Target URL.
 * @param {RequestInit & {retry?: RetryConfig}} [options] Standard fetch options
 *        plus an optional `retry` configuration object.
 * @param {number} [retries=3] Number of **additional** attempts after the first
 *        one. `retries = 3` means up to 4 requests in total; `0` disables retries.
 * @returns {Promise<Response>} The successful (or non-retryable) response.
 * @throws {RetryError|TimeoutError|TypeError|Error}
 *
 * @example
 * const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/v1/items', {
 *   headers: { authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
 *   retry: { baseDelay: 500, maxDelay: 8000, timeout: 5000 },
 * }, 4);
 *
 * @example
 * // Non-idempotent endpoint that is safe to replay (has an idempotency key):
 * await fetchWithRetry('/api/payments', {
 *   method: 'POST',
 *   body: JSON.stringify(payload),
 *   headers: { 'idempotency-key': key, 'content-type': 'application/json' },
 *   retry: { idempotentOnly: false },
 * }, 3);
 */
export async function fetchWithRetry(url, options = {}, retries = 3) {
  if (!Number.isFinite(retries) || retries < 0) {
    throw new TypeError(`retries must be a non-negative finite number, got ${retries}`);
  }

  const { retry: retryConfig = {}, signal: userSignal, ...init } = options ?? {};

  const config = {
    baseDelay: 300,
    maxDelay: 30_000,
    factor: 2,
    jitter: true,
    timeout: 10_000,
    retryableStatuses: new Set(DEFAULT_RETRYABLE_STATUSES),
    idempotentOnly: true,
    respectRetryAfter: true,
    onRetry: null,
    logger: console,
    ...retryConfig,
  };

  const retryableStatuses =
    config.retryableStatuses instanceof Set
      ? config.retryableStatuses
      : new Set(config.retryableStatuses ?? DEFAULT_RETRYABLE_STATUSES);

  const method = String(init.method ?? 'GET').toUpperCase();
  let maxAttempts = Math.floor(retries) + 1;

  // Guard against replaying non-idempotent requests.
  if (config.idempotentOnly && maxAttempts > 1 && !IDEMPOTENT_METHODS.has(method)) {
    config.logger?.warn?.(
      `[fetchWithRetry] ${method} is not idempotent; not retrying. ` +
        `Set { retry: { idempotentOnly: false } } if the endpoint is safe to replay.`,
    );
    maxAttempts = 1;
  }

  let lastError;

  for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= maxAttempts; attempt++) {
    let response;

    try {
      response = await attemptFetch(url, init, config, userSignal);
    } catch (error) {
      // The caller cancelled: stop immediately, do not retry.
      if (userSignal?.aborted) throw error;

      lastError = error;
      if (attempt === maxAttempts) throw error;

      const delay = computeDelay(attempt - 1, config);
      await notify(config, { attempt, delay, error, url: String(url) });
      await sleep(delay, userSignal);
      continue;
    }

    // We have a response: is it a transient failure?
    if (!retryableStatuses.has(response.status)) {
      return response; // Success or a permanent client error — caller decides.
    }

    if (attempt === maxAttempts) {
      // Out of attempts: hand the last response back inside the error.
      throw new RetryError(
        `Request to ${url} failed with status ${response.status} after ${attempt} attempt(s)`,
        { response, attempts: attempt },
      );
    }

    const serverDelay = config.respectRetryAfter ? retryAfterMs(response) : null;
    const delay = Math.min(serverDelay ?? computeDelay(attempt - 1, config), config.maxDelay);

    // Release the connection: we are going to discard this body anyway.
    await discardBody(response);

    await notify(config, { attempt, delay, response, url: String(url) });
    await sleep(delay, userSignal);
  }

  /* istanbul ignore next — the loop always returns or throws. */
  throw lastError ?? new Error('fetchWithRetry: exhausted attempts without a result');
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Helpers                                                                    */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/**
 * Perform a single fetch attempt with an optional per-attempt timeout.
 * @returns {Promise<Response>}
 */
async function attemptFetch(url, init, config, userSignal) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  let timedOut = false;

  const onUserAbort = () => controller.abort(userSignal?.reason);
  if (userSignal) {
    if (userSignal.aborted) throw userSignal.reason ?? abortError();
    userSignal.addEventListener('abort', onUserAbort, { once: true });
  }

  const timer =
    config.timeout > 0
      ? setTimeout(() => {
          timedOut = true;
          controller.abort();
        }, config.timeout)
      : null;

  try {
    return await fetch(url, { ...init, signal: controller.signal });
  } catch (error) {
    if (timedOut) throw new TimeoutError(config.timeout, error);
    throw error;
  } finally {
    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
    userSignal?.removeEventListener('abort', onUserAbort);
  }
}

/**
 * Exponential backoff with "equal jitter": half the delay is fixed, half random.
 * @param {number} attempt Zero-based index of the attempt that just failed.
 */
function computeDelay(attempt, config) {
  const exponential = Math.min(config.maxDelay, config.baseDelay * config.factor ** attempt);
  if (!config.jitter) return Math.round(exponential);
  const half = exponential / 2;
  return Math.round(half + Math.random() * half);
}

/**
 * Parse a `Retry-After` header (delta-seconds or HTTP-date) into milliseconds.
 * @returns {number|null} `null` when the header is absent or unparsable.
 */
function retryAfterMs(response) {
  const header = response.headers?.get?.('retry-after');
  if (!header) return null;

  const seconds = Number(header);
  if (Number.isFinite(seconds)) return Math.max(0, seconds * 1000);

  const date = Date.parse(header);
  if (!Number.isNaN(date)) return Math.max(0, date - Date.now());

  return null;
}

/** Cancel a response body we are not going to read, so the socket is released. */
async function discardBody(response) {
  try {
    await response.body?.cancel();
  } catch {
    /* the body may already be closed — nothing to do */
  }
}

/** Invoke the user-supplied `onRetry` hook (errors in the hook are swallowed). */
async function notify(config, info) {
  if (typeof config.onRetry !== 'function') return;
  try {
    await config.onRetry(info);
  } catch (hookError) {
    config.logger?.warn?.('[fetchWithRetry] onRetry hook threw:', hookError);
  }
}

/** Promise-based `setTimeout` that rejects early if the signal is aborted. */
function sleep(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      reject(signal.reason ?? abortError());
      return;
    }

    const onAbort = () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(signal.reason ?? abortError());
    };

    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      signal?.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
      resolve();
    }, ms);

    signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}

/** A standard `AbortError`, for environments where `signal.reason` is absent. */
function abortError() {
  return new DOMException('The operation was aborted.', 'AbortError');
}
```

### Quick usage

```js
// Simple: up to 4 total attempts, default backoff.
const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data');

// Full control.
const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data', {
  headers: { authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
  signal: AbortSignal.timeout(60_000), // cancels the whole operation
  retry: {
    baseDelay: 500,
    maxDelay: 10_000,
    factor: 2,
    timeout: 5_000,          // per attempt
    onRetry: ({ attempt, delay, error, response }) =>
      console.warn(`attempt ${attempt} failed, retrying in ${delay}ms`, error ?? response?.status),
  },
}, 5);
```

### Design notes / trade-offs

- **Which failures are retried.** Network errors and `408/425/429/5xx` are transient. Other `4xx` responses are returned to the caller rather than retried, since repeating them cannot succeed.
- **Jitter.** Without randomization, a fleet of clients that fails at the same moment retries at the same moment ("thundering herd"). Equal jitter keeps a deterministic floor while still spreading load.
- **`Retry-After`.** For rate limiting, the server's instruction beats our own backoff, so it takes precedence (still capped by `maxDelay`).
- **Idempotency.** Replaying a `POST` can double-charge or double-submit. The default guard refuses to retry non-idempotent methods and logs a warning; opt in explicitly when your endpoint supports it.
- **Timeouts vs. cancellation.** A per-attempt timeout is treated as a retryable failure; an external `AbortSignal` abort is treated as "stop now" and propagates immediately.
- **Body handling.** A response we are about to discard is cancelled to free the connection; the final failing response is left intact inside `RetryError.response` so you can read the error payload.

Possible extensions if you need them: a total time budget (`maxTotalTime`), a circuit breaker in front of the function, or retrying on `Retry-After` even for `4xx` codes other than `429`.

## `fetchWithRetry`

A drop-in wrapper around `fetch` that retries failed requests using exponential backoff with jitter.

```js
/**
 * Fetch a URL with automatic retries and exponential backoff.
 *
 * @param {string|URL} url - The resource to request.
 * @param {RequestInit & {retry?: RetryOptions}} [options] - Standard `fetch`
 *   options, plus an optional `retry` property (removed before the request is sent).
 * @param {number} [retries] - Number of *additional* attempts after the first.
 *   Defaults to `options.retry?.retries ?? 3`. `0` disables retrying.
 *
 * @typedef {object} RetryOptions
 * @property {number} [retries=3]        Max retries after the initial attempt.
 * @property {number} [baseDelay=300]    Delay before the first retry, in ms.
 * @property {number} [maxDelay=30000]   Upper bound for any single delay, in ms.
 * @property {number} [factor=2]         Backoff multiplier between attempts.
 * @property {number} [jitter=0.5]        Randomization: 0 = none, 1 = full.
 * @property {number[]} [statusCodes]     Response statuses to retry.
 * @property {(err: Error) => boolean} [shouldRetry] - Custom retry predicate.
 * @property {AbortSignal} [signal]      Caller-controlled cancellation.
 *
 * @returns {Promise<Response>} The first successful response.
 * @throws {Error} The last error encountered if all attempts fail.
 *
 * @example
 * const res = await fetchWithRetry('/api/data', { retry: { retries: 5 } });
 *
 * @example
 * // Retry only on 5xx, with a 10 s budget
 * await fetchWithRetry(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(10_000) }, 4);
 */
export async function fetchWithRetry(url, options = {}, retries) {
  const {
    retry: retryOptions = {},
    ...init
  } = options;

  const {
    retries: defaultRetries = 3,
    baseDelay = 300,
    maxDelay = 30_000,
    factor = 2,
    jitter = 0.5,
    statusCodes = [408, 425, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504],
    shouldRetry,
  } = retryOptions;

  const maxRetries = retries ?? defaultRetries;

  let attempt = 0;
  let lastError;

  for (;;) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(url, init);

      // A response that is "successful" is returned as-is; the caller decides
      // whether to parse it. Only retry statuses that indicate a transient fault.
      const retryableStatus = statusCodes.includes(response.status);
      if (!retryableStatus || attempt >= maxRetries) {
        return response;
      }

      // Consume the body so the connection can be reused, then retry.
      await response.body?.cancel?.().catch(() => {});
      lastError = new Error(
        `Request failed with status ${response.status} (${response.statusText})`
      );
      lastError.status = response.status;
    } catch (error) {
      // Caller-initiated aborts are never retried.
      if (init.signal?.aborted) throw error;

      lastError = error;
      if (attempt >= maxRetries) throw error;
      if (shouldRetry && !shouldRetry(error)) throw error;
    }

    const delay = computeDelay(attempt, { baseDelay, factor, maxDelay, jitter });
    attempt += 1;
    await sleep(delay, init.signal);
  }
}

/**
 * Compute the backoff delay for a given attempt (0-based).
 * Delay = min(baseDelay * factor^attempt, maxDelay), then randomized by ±jitter.
 *
 * @param {number} attempt
 * @param {{baseDelay: number, factor: number, maxDelay: number, jitter: number}} cfg
 * @returns {number} Delay in milliseconds.
 */
function computeDelay(attempt, { baseDelay, factor, maxDelay, jitter }) {
  const exponential = Math.min(baseDelay * factor ** attempt, maxDelay);
  if (jitter <= 0) return exponential;
  const spread = exponential * jitter;
  // Uniform in [exponential - spread, exponential + spread], clamped at 0.
  return Math.max(0, Math.round(exponential - spread + Math.random() * spread * 2));
}

/**
 * Promise-based sleep that rejects if the signal is aborted.
 *
 * @param {number} ms
 * @param {AbortSignal} [signal]
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
function sleep(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      reject(signal.reason ?? new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
      return;
    }
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      signal?.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
      resolve();
    }, ms);
    function onAbort() {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(signal.reason ?? new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
    }
    signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}
```

### Design notes

- **Exponential backoff with jitter.** Delay grows as `baseDelay * factor^attempt`, capped at `maxDelay`. Jitter (±50% by default) prevents a thundering herd when many clients fail at the same moment.
- **Retryable statuses.** Only transient codes (429, 5xx, etc.) are retried; a `404` is returned immediately since retrying won't change it.
- **Body cleanup.** A failed response's body is cancelled before retrying so the socket isn't leaked.
- **Abort handling.** An external `AbortSignal` cancels both the in-flight request and the backoff sleep, and aborts are never retried.
- **Errors vs. responses.** A final failing *response* is returned to the caller (so you can inspect `status`); a final failing *network error* is thrown.

### Usage

```js
// Simple: 3 retries, default backoff
const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data');

// Custom policy
const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data', {
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
  retry: {
    retries: 5,
    baseDelay: 500,
    maxDelay: 10_000,
    statusCodes: [429, 500, 503],
    shouldRetry: (err) => err.name !== 'TypeError', // don't retry DNS failures
  },
  signal: AbortSignal.timeout(15_000),
});
```

## `fetchWithRetry`

```js
/**
 * Status codes that are treated as *transient* — i.e. worth retrying.
 * 408 Request Timeout, 425 Too Early, 429 Too Many Requests,
 * 500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable,
 * 504 Gateway Timeout.
 * @type {Set<number>}
 */
const DEFAULT_RETRY_STATUSES = new Set([408, 425, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504]);

/**
 * Error thrown when the server keeps answering with a retryable status code
 * and all attempts have been exhausted. The last `Response` is attached so
 * callers can inspect the status, headers and body.
 */
export class HttpError extends Error {
  /** @param {Response} response */
  constructor(response) {
    super(`Request failed with status ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`.trim());
    this.name = 'HttpError';
    this.status = response.status;
    this.response = response;
  }
}

/**
 * Sleep for `ms` milliseconds, rejecting early if `signal` is aborted.
 *
 * @param {number} ms
 * @param {AbortSignal} [signal]
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
function sleep(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      reject(signal.reason ?? new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
      return;
    }
    const onAbort = () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(signal.reason ?? new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
    };
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      signal?.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
      resolve();
    }, ms);
    signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}

/**
 * Fetch a resource, retrying transient failures with exponential backoff.
 *
 * A request is retried when
 *   - the network call throws (DNS failure, connection reset, socket hang up, …), or
 *   - the response status is retryable (see `retryOnStatus`, default: 408/425/429/5xx).
 *
 * The delay before attempt *n* (0-based) is `baseDelay * 2 ** n`, capped at
 * `maxDelay`. With `jitter` enabled (default) the actual delay is uniformly
 * random in `[0, cap]`, which avoids a "thundering herd" when many clients
 * fail at the same moment. A `Retry-After` header is honoured when present.
 *
 * @param {string | URL} url                     Resource to request.
 * @param {RequestInit & {
 *     baseDelay?: number,                       Initial backoff in ms (default 300).
 *     maxDelay?: number,                        Upper bound for a single delay in ms (default 30000).
 *     jitter?: boolean,                         Randomise the delay (default true).
 *     timeout?: number,                         Per-attempt timeout in ms; 0 disables (default 0).
 *     retryOnStatus?: (status: number) => boolean,
 *                                               Decide whether a status is retryable.
 *     respectRetryAfter?: boolean,              Honour the `Retry-After` header (default true).
 *     onRetry?: (info: { attempt: number, delay: number, error?: Error, status?: number }) => void,
 *                                               Callback invoked before each retry (useful for logging).
 *   }} [options]                               Standard `fetch` init plus the retry knobs above.
 * @param {number} [retries=3]                  Number of *additional* attempts after the first
 *                                               (so `retries: 3` means up to 4 requests in total).
 * @returns {Promise<Response>}                 The first non-retryable response.
 * @throws {HttpError}                          If the last attempt returned a retryable status.
 * @throws {Error}                              The last network/abort error if every attempt failed.
 *
 * @example
 * const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data', {
 *   headers: { accept: 'application/json' },
 *   timeout: 5_000,
 * }, 5);
 * const data = await res.json();
 */
export async function fetchWithRetry(url, options = {}, retries = 3) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(retries) || retries < 0) {
    throw new TypeError('`retries` must be a non-negative integer');
  }

  const {
    baseDelay = 300,
    maxDelay = 30_000,
    jitter = true,
    timeout = 0,
    retryOnStatus = (status) => DEFAULT_RETRY_STATUSES.has(status),
    respectRetryAfter = true,
    onRetry,
    signal,
    ...init
  } = options;

  /**
   * Compute the backoff delay for the given attempt.
   * @param {number} attempt 0-based index of the attempt that just failed.
   * @param {Response} [response]
   * @returns {number} delay in ms
   */
  const computeDelay = (attempt, response) => {
    if (respectRetryAfter && response) {
      const header = response.headers?.get?.('retry-after');
      if (header) {
        const seconds = Number(header);
        if (Number.isFinite(seconds)) return Math.min(Math.max(seconds * 1000, 0), maxDelay);
        const date = Date.parse(header);
        if (!Number.isNaN(date)) return Math.min(Math.max(date - Date.now(), 0), maxDelay);
      }
    }
    const capped = Math.min(maxDelay, baseDelay * 2 ** attempt);
    return jitter ? Math.random() * capped : capped;
  };

  /**
   * Perform a single fetch attempt, optionally with a per-attempt timeout.
   * @returns {Promise<Response>}
   */
  const attemptFetch = async () => {
    if (!timeout) return fetch(url, { ...init, signal });

    const controller = new AbortController();
    const timer = setTimeout(
      () => controller.abort(new DOMException('Request timed out', 'TimeoutError')),
      timeout,
    );
    const onExternalAbort = () => controller.abort(signal.reason);
    if (signal) {
      if (signal.aborted) controller.abort(signal.reason);
      else signal.addEventListener('abort', onExternalAbort, { once: true });
    }
    try {
      return await fetch(url, { ...init, signal: controller.signal });
    } finally {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      signal?.removeEventListener('abort', onExternalAbort);
    }
  };

  let lastError;

  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= retries; attempt++) {
    const isLast = attempt === retries;

    try {
      const response = await attemptFetch();

      if (!retryOnStatus(response.status)) return response;

      // Retryable status: remember it and (if attempts remain) back off.
      lastError = new HttpError(response);

      if (isLast) throw lastError;

      // Release the connection so the socket can be reused.
      try { await response.body?.cancel?.(); } catch { /* ignore */ }

      const delay = computeDelay(attempt, response);
      onRetry?.({ attempt: attempt + 1, delay, status: response.status });
      await sleep(delay, signal);
    } catch (error) {
      // Never retry a request that the caller explicitly aborted.
      if (signal?.aborted) throw error;
      if (error instanceof HttpError) throw error; // already handled above

      lastError = error;
      if (isLast) throw error;

      const delay = computeDelay(attempt);
      onRetry?.({ attempt: attempt + 1, delay, error });
      await sleep(delay, signal);
    }
  }

  /* istanbul ignore next — the loop always returns or throws. */
  throw lastError;
}
```

### Usage

```js
// Basic: 3 retries (4 attempts total) with exponential backoff + jitter.
const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/users', {
  headers: { accept: 'application/json' },
  timeout: 5_000,
  onRetry: ({ attempt, delay, error, status }) =>
    console.warn(`retry #${attempt} in ${Math.round(delay)}ms`, error?.message ?? status),
}, 3);

if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
const users = await res.json();
```

### Notes / caveats

- **`retries` counts extra attempts**: `retries: 3` ⇒ up to 4 HTTP requests.
- **Aborting is respected**: if `options.signal` is aborted, the function rejects immediately and never retries.
- **Timeouts are per attempt**, not for the whole operation; the total wall-clock time is roughly `retries × (timeout + delay)`.
- **Only transient failures are retried.** A `400`/`401`/`404` is returned as-is so you can handle it.
- **Idempotency**: retrying is safe for `GET`/`HEAD`/`PUT`/`DELETE`. For `POST`/`PATCH`, make sure the endpoint is idempotent or carries an idempotency key — otherwise you may create duplicates.
- **Request bodies must be replayable**: pass a string/`Buffer`/`FormData`/`URLSearchParams`, not a one-shot stream (a consumed stream cannot be re-sent).

## `fetchWithRetry` — JavaScript (Node 18+ / modern browsers)

```js
/**
 * HTTP status codes that are considered *transient* and therefore safe to retry.
 * Everything else (e.g. 400, 401, 403, 404) is returned immediately.
 */
const RETRYABLE_STATUS_CODES = new Set([408, 425, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504]);

const DEFAULT_BASE_DELAY_MS = 500;   // First backoff delay
const DEFAULT_MAX_DELAY_MS = 30_000;  // Upper bound for a single backoff delay

/**
 * Performs an HTTP request and automatically retries transient failures using
 * exponential backoff with jitter.
 *
 * ## Retry policy
 * - A request is retried when it fails with a **network error** (e.g. `ECONNRESET`,
 *   DNS failure) or returns a **retryable status code** (408, 425, 429, 5xx).
 * - The delay before retry *n* (0-based) is `baseDelayMs * 2^n`, capped at
 *   `maxDelayMs` and randomized by "equal jitter" (a random value in
 *   `[delay / 2, delay]`) so that many clients do not retry in lockstep.
 * - If the server sends a `Retry-After` header (on a 429/503), that value is
 *   honoured instead of the computed backoff.
 * - `retries` counts *additional* attempts, so `retries = 3` means up to
 *   **4 total requests**.
 *
 * ## Error handling
 * - Non-retryable responses (e.g. `404`) are **returned as-is**; inspect
 *   `response.ok` / `response.status` yourself.
 * - If the last attempt fails with a network error, the error is **thrown**
 *   (the original error, with the attempt count available on `error.attempt`).
 * - A caller-initiated abort (`options.signal`) is **never retried**; the
 *   abort reason is rethrown immediately.
 *
 * @param {string} url                       The URL to request.
 * @param {RequestInit & {
 *     timeoutMs?: number,
 *     baseDelayMs?: number,
 *     maxDelayMs?: number
 *   }} [options={}]
 *        Standard `fetch` options (`method`, `headers`, `body`, `signal`, …) plus:
 *        - `timeoutMs`  – per-attempt timeout in ms (default: no timeout).
 *        - `baseDelayMs`– base backoff delay in ms (default: 500).
 *        - `maxDelayMs` – maximum backoff delay in ms (default: 30000).
 * @param {number} [retries=3]               Number of *retries* after the first attempt.
 * @returns {Promise<Response>}              The last response received.
 * @throws {Error}                           The last network/timeout error if every attempt failed.
 * @throws {AbortError | DOMException}       If the caller's `signal` was aborted.
 *
 * @example
 * const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data', {
 *   method: 'POST',
 *   headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
 *   body: JSON.stringify({ hello: 'world' }),
 *   timeoutMs: 5_000,
 * }, 3);
 *
 * if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
 * const data = await res.json();
 */
export async function fetchWithRetry(url, options = {}, retries = 3) {
  const {
    timeoutMs,
    baseDelayMs = DEFAULT_BASE_DELAY_MS,
    maxDelayMs = DEFAULT_MAX_DELAY_MS,
    ...init
  } = options;

  const maxAttempts = Math.max(0, Math.trunc(retries)) + 1;
  const requestInit = withTimeout(init, timeoutMs);

  let lastError;

  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < maxAttempts; attempt++) {
    const isLastAttempt = attempt === maxAttempts - 1;

    try {
      const response = await fetch(url, requestInit);

      // Success, a permanent failure, or no attempts left → hand it to the caller.
      if (!RETRYABLE_STATUS_CODES.has(response.status) || isLastAttempt) {
        return response;
      }

      // Retryable status: prefer the server's `Retry-After`, else back off exponentially.
      const retryAfterMs = parseRetryAfter(response.headers.get('retry-after'));
      const delayMs = retryAfterMs ?? computeBackoffDelay(attempt, baseDelayMs, maxDelayMs);

      await discardBody(response); // Release the socket so the retry isn't blocked.
      await sleep(delayMs, requestInit.signal);
    } catch (error) {
      // A caller-initiated abort must never be retried.
      if (requestInit.signal?.aborted && !isTimeoutError(error)) {
        throw error;
      }

      lastError = error;
      if (isLastAttempt) break;

      await sleep(computeBackoffDelay(attempt, baseDelayMs, maxDelayMs), requestInit.signal);
    }
  }

  // Every attempt failed with a network/timeout error.
  if (lastError) {
    lastError.attempt = maxAttempts;
    throw lastError;
  }
  throw new Error(`Request to ${url} failed after ${maxAttempts} attempt(s).`);
}

/**
 * Computes an exponential-backoff delay with equal jitter.
 * @param {number} attempt      0-based attempt index.
 * @param {number} baseDelayMs  Base delay.
 * @param {number} maxDelayMs   Maximum delay.
 * @returns {number} Delay in milliseconds.
 */
function computeBackoffDelay(attempt, baseDelayMs, maxDelayMs) {
  const exponential = baseDelayMs * 2 ** attempt;
  const capped = Math.min(exponential, maxDelayMs);
  return Math.round(capped / 2 + Math.random() * (capped / 2));
}

/**
 * Parses a `Retry-After` header (delay-seconds or an HTTP date).
 * @param {string|null} value
 * @returns {number|null} Delay in milliseconds, or `null` if not parseable.
 */
function parseRetryAfter(value) {
  if (!value) return null;

  const seconds = Number(value);
  if (Number.isFinite(seconds)) return Math.max(0, seconds * 1000);

  const date = Date.parse(value);
  if (!Number.isNaN(date)) return Math.max(0, date - Date.now());

  return null;
}

/**
 * Waits for `ms` milliseconds, rejecting early if `signal` is aborted.
 * @param {number} ms
 * @param {AbortSignal} [signal]
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
function sleep(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      reject(signal.reason ?? new DOMException('The operation was aborted.', 'AbortError'));
      return;
    }

    const onAbort = () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(signal.reason ?? new DOMException('The operation was aborted.', 'AbortError'));
    };

    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      signal?.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
      resolve();
    }, ms);

    signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}

/**
 * Adds a per-attempt timeout signal, combined with any caller-supplied signal.
 * @param {RequestInit} init
 * @param {number} [timeoutMs]
 * @returns {RequestInit}
 */
function withTimeout(init, timeoutMs) {
  if (!timeoutMs || timeoutMs <= 0) return init;

  const timeoutSignal = AbortSignal.timeout(timeoutMs);
  if (!init.signal) return { ...init, signal: timeoutSignal };

  // Combine both signals without relying on `AbortSignal.any` (not everywhere yet).
  const controller = new AbortController();
  for (const signal of [init.signal, timeoutSignal]) {
    if (signal.aborted) {
      controller.abort(signal.reason);
      break;
    }
    signal.addEventListener('abort', () => controller.abort(signal.reason), { once: true });
  }
  return { ...init, signal: controller.signal };
}

/**
 * Cancels an unused response body so the connection can be reused.
 * @param {Response} response
 */
async function discardBody(response) {
  try {
    await response.body?.cancel();
  } catch {
    /* ignore — the body may already be closed */
  }
}

/**
 * @param {unknown} error
 * @returns {boolean} `true` for timeout errors (which *are* retried).
 */
function isTimeoutError(error) {
  return error?.name === 'TimeoutError';
}
```

### Usage

```js
// Basic: up to 4 total attempts, ~500 ms → 1 s → 2 s backoff
const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/users', {}, 3);

// With a timeout and a caller-controlled abort
const controller = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 10_000);

const res2 = await fetchWithRetry(
  'https://api.example.com/slow',
  { signal: controller.signal, timeoutMs: 3_000 },
  5
);
```

### Notes / trade-offs

- **Non-2xx responses are not thrown.** The function returns the last `Response`; check `res.ok` yourself. Only network errors and transient statuses trigger retries.
- **Retries are not idempotent-safe by default.** Retrying a `POST` can duplicate side effects — pass an `Idempotency-Key` header if the API supports it, or set `retries = 0` for non-idempotent requests.
- **Jitter** prevents a thundering herd when many clients hit the same failing service at once.
- **Timeouts are per attempt**, so the total wall-clock time is roughly `attempts × timeoutMs` plus backoff delays.

## `fetchWithRetry`

```js
/**
 * Default configuration (override by editing these constants or by passing
 * your own values through a wrapper around this function).
 */
const DEFAULT_RETRIES = 3;      // additional attempts after the first one
const BASE_DELAY_MS = 500;      // delay before the first retry
const MAX_DELAY_MS = 30_000;    // upper bound for any single delay
const JITTER_RATIO = 0.5;       // 50% of each delay is randomised

/**
 * Status codes that are worth retrying:
 *  - 408 Request Timeout
 *  - 425 Too Early
 *  - 429 Too Many Requests
 *  - any 5xx Server Error
 * Other 4xx responses are *not* retried: repeating the request would not
 * change the outcome.
 */
const RETRYABLE_STATUSES = new Set([408, 425, 429]);

/**
 * Performs a `fetch` request and retries failed attempts with exponential backoff.
 *
 * A request is considered *failed* when it throws a network error (DNS failure,
 * connection reset, TLS error, ...) or when the server answers with a retryable
 * status code (see `RETRYABLE_STATUSES`).
 *
 * Backoff: the delay before retry number `n` (0-based) is
 * `min(BASE_DELAY_MS * 2 ** n, MAX_DELAY_MS)`, with half of that value
 * randomised ("equal jitter") so that many clients hitting a failing server
 * do not all retry at the same instant. If the response contains a
 * `Retry-After` header, that value is used instead (capped at `MAX_DELAY_MS`).
 *
 * Semantics:
 *  - Resolves with the `Response` of the first successful attempt, or with the
 *    response of the final attempt if the server kept returning a retryable
 *    status. Check `response.ok` to detect HTTP-level failures.
 *  - Rejects with the last network error if no attempt ever produced a
 *    response, or immediately with the abort reason if `options.signal` aborts.
 *
 * Caveats:
 *  - Retrying is only safe for idempotent requests (GET/HEAD/PUT/DELETE) or for
 *    requests that carry an idempotency key. A POST may be executed twice if
 *    the server received it but the response was lost.
 *  - The body of a retried response is discarded (the connection is released),
 *    so only the *final* response body is readable.
 *
 * @param {string | URL} url              The resource to request.
 * @param {RequestInit} [options={}]      Options forwarded verbatim to `fetch`
 *                                        (method, headers, body, signal, ...).
 * @param {number} [retries=3]            Maximum number of *additional* attempts
 *                                        after the initial one. `0` disables retries.
 * @returns {Promise<Response>}           The response of the first successful (or last) attempt.
 * @throws {TypeError}                    If `retries` is not a non-negative integer.
 * @throws {Error | DOMException}         If every attempt failed with a network error,
 *                                        or if the request was aborted.
 *
 * @example
 * const res = await fetchWithRetry('/api/data', { signal: controller.signal }, 5);
 * if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
 * const data = await res.json();
 */
async function fetchWithRetry(url, options = {}, retries = DEFAULT_RETRIES) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(retries) || retries < 0) {
    throw new TypeError('`retries` must be a non-negative integer');
  }

  for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
    // Bail out early if the caller already cancelled the request.
    throwIfAborted(options.signal);

    let response = null;
    let networkError = null;

    try {
      response = await fetch(url, options);
    } catch (err) {
      networkError = err;
    }

    // --- Network error -----------------------------------------------------
    if (networkError) {
      // An abort is not a transient failure: stop immediately.
      if (options.signal?.aborted) throw abortReason(options.signal);
      if (attempt >= retries) throw networkError;

      await sleep(computeDelay(attempt), options.signal);
      continue;
    }

    // --- HTTP response -----------------------------------------------------
    const retryable = RETRYABLE_STATUSES.has(response.status) || response.status >= 500;

    if (!retryable || attempt >= retries) {
      return response; // caller decides what to do with a non-ok response
    }

    const delay = computeDelay(attempt, response);
    await discardBody(response); // free the socket instead of leaking it
    await sleep(delay, options.signal);
  }
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ helpers */

/**
 * Computes the delay (ms) before the next attempt.
 * @param {number} attempt Zero-based index of the attempt that just failed.
 * @param {Response} [response] The failed response, used to honour `Retry-After`.
 * @returns {number} Delay in milliseconds.
 */
function computeDelay(attempt, response) {
  const retryAfter = parseRetryAfter(response?.headers?.get?.('retry-after'));
  if (retryAfter !== null) {
    return Math.min(retryAfter, MAX_DELAY_MS);
  }

  const exponential = Math.min(BASE_DELAY_MS * 2 ** attempt, MAX_DELAY_MS);
  const fixed = exponential * (1 - JITTER_RATIO);
  return Math.round(fixed + Math.random() * (exponential - fixed));
}

/**
 * Parses a `Retry-After` header (delta-seconds or HTTP-date).
 * @param {string | null | undefined} value
 * @returns {number | null} Milliseconds to wait, or `null` if unparsable.
 */
function parseRetryAfter(value) {
  if (!value) return null;

  const seconds = Number(value);
  if (!Number.isNaN(seconds)) return Math.max(0, seconds * 1000);

  const timestamp = Date.parse(value);
  if (Number.isNaN(timestamp)) return null;
  return Math.max(0, timestamp - Date.now());
}

/**
 * Promise-based `setTimeout` that can be cancelled through an `AbortSignal`.
 * @param {number} ms
 * @param {AbortSignal} [signal]
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
function sleep(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      reject(abortReason(signal));
      return;
    }

    const onAbort = () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(abortReason(signal));
    };

    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      signal?.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
      resolve();
    }, ms);

    signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}

/**
 * Cancels the body of a response that will not be used, so the underlying
 * connection can be released instead of being kept alive.
 * @param {Response} response
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
async function discardBody(response) {
  try {
    await response.body?.cancel?.();
  } catch {
    /* the body may already be closed — nothing to clean up */
  }
}

/**
 * @param {AbortSignal} [signal]
 * @returns {Error | DOMException} The reason the signal was aborted.
 */
function abortReason(signal) {
  return signal?.reason ?? new DOMException('The operation was aborted.', 'AbortError');
}

/**
 * Throws if the given signal has already been aborted.
 * @param {AbortSignal} [signal]
 */
function throwIfAborted(signal) {
  if (signal?.aborted) throw abortReason(signal);
}
```

### Usage

```js
// Simple GET with 3 retries (4 attempts total)
const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://example.com/api/data');

// Custom options + more retries, with cancellation support
const controller = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 10_000);

try {
  const res = await fetchWithRetry(
    'https://example.com/api/data',
    { method: 'GET', headers: { Accept: 'application/json' }, signal: controller.signal },
    5
  );
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
  console.log(await res.json());
} catch (err) {
  if (err.name === 'AbortError') console.log('Request cancelled');
  else console.error('All attempts failed:', err);
}
```

**Design notes**

- **What is retried:** network errors, `408`, `425`, `429`, and `5xx`. Other `4xx` responses fail fast.
- **Backoff:** `500ms → 1s → 2s → 4s …`, capped at 30s, with 50% jitter to avoid a thundering herd; a `Retry-After` header takes precedence.
- **Result contract:** resolves with a `Response` (check `response.ok`), rejects only on network failure or abort — so it stays a drop-in replacement for `fetch`.
- **Safety:** because a POST can be processed twice if the response is lost in transit, restrict retries to idempotent requests or use idempotency keys.
